 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50358314": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T12:48:52.167Z",
  "bytes": 10556,
  "text": " Show HN: Kairos – Open-source risk and fraud decisioning platform | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login Show HN: Kairos – Open-source risk and fraud decisioning platform ( github.com/finic-ai ) 104 points by ayanb9440 1 day ago | hide | past | favorite | 34 comments Hey HN, Ayan here from Finic. We’re launching Kairos, an open source alternative to Sift, Sardine, and other real-time fraud decisioning platforms. Kairos addresses two of the biggest problems facing traditional decisioning tools: a lack of labeled data to train on and dealing with fraudsters that are constantly changing their tactics. 1. Traditional fraud solutions use ML models such as XGBoost to detect fraud, but these models are dependent on quality labeled data which many businesses do not have. We address this by using “System One” decision models in additional to traditional models such as XGBoost and logistic regression. This allows you to incorporate plain language questions such as “Is this a throwaway email” into your decisioning, bypassing the need for large amounts of labeled data. This also allows you to capture aspects of fraud that are more unstructured, which is something that XGBoost models struggle with. 2. Fraud teams are facing off with live players that are constantly changing their behavior to bypass the detection logic they have in place. You can train a detection model or define a ruleset once, but it will become obsolete in a matter of weeks as fraudsters figure out what your detection logic is. This means that you need a team regularly training models and updating rules based on new fraud patterns. Typically this takes a large data science/ML team. Kairos addresses this by introducing an MCP server, so that your agent (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, etc.) can define a decision workflow, train models on your data, and pick the variant that performs best. You can self-host Kairos with Docker or use the hosted version at https://kairos.finic.ai help \n \n factotvm 1 day ago | next [–] \n I love that this is open source, thank you. I'm not a fan of TypeScript on Node for this solution space. For a payment gateway, I'd want a program that can't modify itself from external sources. Programs in this runtime can rewrite themselves while running, and the Dockerfile doesn't lock it down. So as it stands, nothing at the runtime level prevents prototype pollution. And a config defect is the kind of bug that keeps on giving. reply bradknowles 1 day ago | prev | next [–] \n This isn’t really a new problem. Back in 1995, I was the Sr. Internet Mail Administrator at AOL. We were doing everything we could to fight the spammers. We found that we would work on a new technique over a period of weeks or months, and then they would find a way to work around our technique in a single weekend. Rinse and repeat. The only thing that has changed is that the market for scammers has moved, and they’ve gotten a lot better and faster at adapting to your protection techniques. reply citguru 20 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n True, but still a big issue today reply edude03 1 day ago | prev | next [–] \n Not something I need but super cool that this is both open source and shows the realtime flow. reply tehsurf 1 day ago | prev | next [–] \n Thanks for showing Keiros! This looks very promising! Could you explain how \"System One\" differs from the traditional, rule based, approach? Sticking to your example: How would the question 'Is this a throwaway email?' be translated into Kairos business logic? When using the MCP Server: How does Claude develop such models, e.g. feature engineering? Is this an MLOps pipeline behind an MCP? reply agileAlligator 1 day ago | parent | next [–] \n System one is just a Jev API Call presumably. Or one of the open source alternatives to Jev.\n https://typesafe.ai/blog/introducing-system-one-models-and-j... reply citguru 1 day ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Actually while similar to Jev and uses the popularized architecture by Jev, Kairos uses an underlying model called Grayson trained by Finic but for Fraud. You can check it out - https://finic.ai/ reply tehsurf 1 day ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Thanks! reply tensegrist 1 day ago | prev | next [–] \n this is a new one https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49610571 reply bobbiechen 1 day ago | parent | next [–] \n Referring to \"decisioning\", which is unfortunately a real term in this space. Don't get me started on people who use \"decision on\" to mean \"enforce a decision\" (i.e. by blocking an action) reply wuemeli 1 day ago | prev | next [–] \n Why would you ask a decision model if an email is disposable? There is so much more behind that than the looks of the email. This just seems like yet another video-coded anti-fraud thingi. reply citguru 21 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Not just that. You could ask any more complex questions. Thats just an easy example reply hemankgurram 1 day ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n yea its disposable if you want it that wayyy reply garageguideapp 23 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Can you do anything about Debbie in HR clicking links? reply citguru 20 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Debbie, probably needs more email training reply octoberfranklin 1 day ago | prev | next [–] \n Is anybody else annoyed by made-up words like \"decisioning\" and \"proofing\"? You can't slap \"ing\" on a noun. It's for verbs. Stop nouning. reply marcus_holmes 1 day ago | parent | next [–] \n Language is what people use to communicate, not what's written in the books. It's fluid, and adapts to requirements as the requirements change. Would it be better if they invented a completely new word, with no clue as to what it means, rather than adapt an existing language structure to their purpose, which everyone understands immediately (even if they get grumpy at it)? reply octoberfranklin 13 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n That's the mindset that led to Ebonics. How'd that work out? reply marcus_holmes 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'd never heard of it before. So I read the Wikipedia page on it. From that, I think the main problem is defining AAE (or Ebonics which appears to be a synonym) as a different language. AAE is probably more understandable to the average English speaker than the dialect of NE England [0]. So, to answer your question: I think that the term Ebonics didn't really work out, but otherwise it's going OK. There are multiple different dialects of English, of which AAE (Ebonics) is one, and they all use different words, not all of those words are in the dictionary or used in the manner that the OP is talking about, and they are all English. How do you think it worked out? [0] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geordie reply mh- 1 day ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n yes, but don't blame OP for it. this is effectively a term of art in their field. reply octoberfranklin 1 day ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I blame OP for perpetuating it. And for extending it (to HN) outside their field. reply Messier43 1 day ago | prev [–] \n There's a meta linux distribution with that name already :( https://kairos.io/ reply jonas21 1 day ago | parent | next [–] \n And an identity verification company: https://kairos.com/ And an AI safety organization: https://kairos-project.org/ And a DARPA program: https://www.darpa.mil/research/programs/knowledge-directed-a... Pick any ancient greek word and you'll probably find at least a handful of collisions. reply citguru 1 day ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n There will always be projects that share the same name. You can do quick search on Github: https://github.com/search?q=kairos&type=repositories . However it does not change the significance of what each project is building and the problem they sought to solve. reply giwook 1 day ago | parent | prev [–] \n and...? reply Joel_Mckay 1 day ago | root | parent [–] \n In general, the community tries to avoid/mitigate project naming confusion. It is usually a gentleman's agreement, but sometimes may get nasty with trademarks/copyrights (example: Arduino.) If you are not associated with a core decision team, it is considered nice to avoid confusing the public. In my opinion, gleaning donations is just theft. =3 reply rytill 1 day ago | root | parent [–] \n \"The community\" of every single person that works on software and names things? For which you can speak? reply gnabgib 1 day ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Oh that community! Yeah we banned Joel_Mckay for speaking for us reply Joel_Mckay 1 day ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I wish I had more time to participate in FOSS these days, as there is a backlog of projects I'd like to get done. Now mostly focused on rather boring small stuff... like sub-micron boring... Have a wonderful day =3 reply marcus_holmes 1 day ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n \"we\"? reply Joel_Mckay 1 day ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Are you asking a bad comedian to explain their joke? My only active FOSS project right now is a unpublished 3D volumetric slicer for a home metal printer that won't be public for another 2 to 3 years for various trade reasons. I code mostly for fun these days, and the dude doesn't sound like fun to work around. =3 reply marcus_holmes 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I was trying, but obviously failing, to extend the joke by asking who \"we\" was, as per the old Lone Ranger joke ( https://huntthedevil.wordpress.com/2015/05/08/what-do-you-me... ) I have no FOSS projects, I'm not sure I entirely agree with it all, there's an element of \"getting developers to create free stuff for corporations\". I notice there are no \"FOSS\" architecture plans, or any other professional output. But I digress. Good for you :) reply Joel_Mckay 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It is a personal choice to share commercially non-viable works under Apache 2.0, but I agree FOSS won't normally pay the bills. One can't do a OSS project expecting other people to give a toss, but rather it solves a niche issue you and 5 other hobbyists encountered. =3 reply Joel_Mckay 1 day ago | root | parent | prev [–] \n In general, traditionally there are underappreciated curators that take on the role of keeping a named package or program consistent, and may go though a personal validation process with a OS build organization to provide a chain of accountability for the user-base. The amount of abuse these people endure from general users is a classic rerun. =3 reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
