s a very clear cost-benefit analysis; your revenue is hacking organizations and extorting them for available money, and your costs are developing and deploying attacks. The ROI is outrageously good easily being on the order of 10x to 100x. If it is so easy and so profitable why do not they just hack all of the millions of organizations on the planet with millions of dollars of available cash for trillions of dollars of revenue? Sure, all they have to do is create a trillion dollar revenue commercial organization bootstrapping themselves with no outside investors, loans, or access to financial resources or the financial system. I mean, it took Mark Zuckerberg 20 years and a metric ton of VC investment and public investment to make 200 billion dollars in revenue. So what kind of loser can not make a commercial organization 5x larger with a trillion dollars in revenue in less time with no outside investors? Are they even trying? Just make more revenue than any other commercial organization in the entire world and extract that money in a freely usable form, how hard can that be? They are not there yet, but they are trying. If you look at cybercrime reports you see something like 3x-10x growth year over year. Ransomware reports from a few years ago were showing a 3x year over year growth in number of attacks and simultaneously a 3x year over year growth in ransom amount with the averages being on the order of 100,000 dollars. Chump change for any serious organization, but give that a few years of 3x and that starts to look like real money. However, as it becomes real money it becomes harder and harder to launder it into a usable form. Sure, you could hit someone for 30 million dollars, but how are you going to get that into your bank account? Cryptocurrency helps some, but even there the big money tries to steer away from large overt crime. Maybe you could launder a few tens of billions a year that way, but that is still a far cry from the trillions for everything everywhere. And of course even assuming you can scale your organization 10x year over year it still takes a decade of sustained growth, scaling your hiring and organization across 9 orders of magnitude, to get to billions per year. A literal decade even if you were growing at a outrageous pace for a outrageous amount of time just to reach a fraction of the total addressable market. A decade of that and you are still nowhere near blowing up all the power plants. Give it time, we need a lot of bears to get there. reply bananaquant 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n It feels like this comment section has either gotten collective amnesia or didn't watch Greg KH's video on previous dump and grossly exaggerated claims from Anthropic [0]. From his talk, AIs can't reliably deduplicate or count vulnerabilities, dash for easy and meaningless escape hatches like malicious filesystems, and report the same things as found by everyone else. Indeed, this post literally says:\n\"Within 10 days while the scan was running, we observed over 100 Linux LPEs had been patched or had staging patches from other contributors, with ~300 more being publicly discussed.\" A few ready takeaways: this is just hype. The community is already doing a great job at finding and patching the actual vulns without the attention-desperate big labs. And no, you absolutely do not need to pay them a million dollars each year for the privilege. This behavior is the same thing that OpenAI has been doing to math -- produce tons of slop that are useless to humans. But this time, we don't even get to see how bad it is because they need to hide it to protect our precious little minds. We have got to be afraid though, very afraid. Our field really needs someone like Terry Tao to unambiguously stand up to this behavior and say that they are not welcome to take such dumps on everyone. [0]: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NnV_cWeoo5Q reply QuantumNomad_ 3 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > The community is already doing a great job at finding and patching the actual vulns without the attention-desperate big labs. It is however reasonable to assume that a lot of bugs that are being found by the community these days were found thanks to those people using AI to aid them. See for example what a PostgreSQL developer is saying: > All software contains bugs, and PostgreSQL’s code base is large, complex, and old. Our git history – converted from CVS – now reaches back more than 30 years, and the first code was written a decade before that. There’s a huge amount of code, and that code contains a huge number of bugs. That’s always been true, and it hasn’t stopped PostgreSQL from being popular or successful. What’s different now is that it’s become vastly easier to find those bugs. […] > […] > […] a huge number of people on the Internet are now reporting bugs in PostgreSQL that they found using LLMs, and the PostgreSQL committers have been very, very busy fixing them […] http://rhaas.blogspot.com/2026/10/postgres-19-is-late-and-it... https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50312408 reply bananaquant 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Your argument is literal red herring. Postgres is a different project with different codebase and vastly different-sized security team. Why not look at something like SQLite instead and conclude that things are great? reply QuantumNomad_ 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n All I’m saying is, that it is reasonable to assume that a lot of other people also benefit from AI to find bugs in Linux. The Linux kernel is also a huge amount of code that has been worked on by a lot of people for many many years. There are certain types of bugs that are too obscure to realize are even there for a person alone. And there are types of bugs that even if you see that something is funky it is too time consuming to dig into for a human. I am not saying that the Linux community is not doing great. They are doing a fantastic job, and have been for years, and they are giving all of that away for free too which is nothing short of incredible. What I am saying is that I think AI allows everyone to find even more bugs than was humanly possible without AI. reply bananaquant 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It seems we are in agreement then. It doesn't cost a million dollar a year super-restricted AI to find those bugs and the community is already successfully doing that. reply nas 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I've always considered local escalations to be possible in Linux (i.e. having user access almost certainly means you can get root). However, 948 vulnerabilities is kind of shocking. Hopefully we are getting to the end of the part where it \"gets worse before it gets better\". reply markus_zhang 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n I wonder what is the opinion of people who study and create exploits? Are you happy or not? reply treebeard901 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > Large, complex software projects likely require multiple rounds of scanning and fixing vulnerabilities to “bottom out” latent bugs. Ideally this process is repeated with each subsequent generation of frontier models. This applies to both proprietary and open source software. One of the most important paragraphs from the article. Real implications for software development from a security perspective for new and existing software. Every software developer and vendor has a choice... Submit your code base to a frontier model to have it reviewed and fix all the bugs. Each time a new model is released, this process must be done again. If your company is against submitting your code to the frontier model companies for the many good reasons to avoid doing it, your software will eventually become a target if it isn't already. Then your company will be forced to scan the software and deal with the fallout from getting hacked. If the Linux kernel has this many exploits, and it has been audited more than most other software... Then it seems obvious that all other software is even less secure. reply miki123211 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Have there been significant AI-assisted exploits found against software which is not written in memory-unsafe languages? As models get better and porting costs get lower, maybe it's better to bite the bullet and RRiR instead of having a minor bugpocalypse every 3 months, as each subsequent generation of frontier models finds sev-high CVEs? reply loremium 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Security has been treated by corporations as a cost factor only and therefore ignored for years and now it starts to hurt not having taken care of in the first place. reply lifeisloving 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This just translates to, \"you're going to need to do multiple rounds of scanning so we recommend budgeting $3.6 million a year for buying tokens from us not just $1.2 million. Dont worry just give us a few million dollars and you'll never get hacked, you can cut your security staff in half to pay for it! We definately wont cause more bugs!\" reply hypfer 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > If your company is against submitting your code to the frontier model companies for the many good reasons to avoid doing it, your software will eventually become a target if it isn't already. Yeah yeah we're all going to be left behind if we do not jump off the same cliff everyone else is jumping off. It is wrong to try to take your own lessons from this and do the thing but in a less harmful way. Just do exactly what the labs want you to do. Jesus man reply Luke2642 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Do we need to switch to seL4 type verified kernels? There's no way we can secure millions lines of code, the attack surface is too big? reply minimaxa 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n We are all just sitting ducks, until we have access to these models and enough tokens to pen test our systems. The other issue is GitHub allowing 1000's of black hat open-source exploit malware swarm tools being forked. The future internet is going to be perilous. Within 12 hours, the analyst agents produced 2,308 candidate findings after deduplication. This stage cost $316,549 in API credits. reply perlgeek 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n > We are all just sitting ducks, until we have access to these models and enough tokens to pen test our systems. You don't need SOTA models to pend test your systems. With enough effort put into proper harnesses, you can use good open-weights models and achieve a lot. You can also use coding agents to clear out technical debt, or improve the security architecture. Don't let yourself get paralyzed by lack of access to the latest-and-greatest models. reply solyin 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > We are all just sitting ducks You can the same $20/mo Claude code plan you've been using to build your system to red team attack the system you've been building with it and probe it for holes, and it'll do a decent job of making sure there aren't any glaring issues. reply EmX_3000 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n So... this is what I get out of my trillion dollar stimulus? OpenAI is developing a universal rooting system skeleton key to get at everybody's computers? Slap me if I'm not reading this correctly, but I think that's the gist of it. It's time everybody started their own air-gapped home network with every digital capability you hold dear, I think. That's what he's saying. Imagine somebody breaches their security, or the AI goes schizo, we've seen this so many times in fiction. Finally I have a reason to use Firewire again lol. reply 71bw 6 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Why wouldn’t your network be air-gapped? reply asdff 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You have home wifi or wired ethernet? Sorry but let’s have those without sin cast the first stone. reply zahlman 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > We've developed an autonomous vulnerability discovery, exploitation, and patching pipeline. In one run with our cyber model, it validated and generated patches for 948 local privilege escalation vulnerabilities in Linux at an API cost of $1.2 million. Imagine if open-source organizations other than the Linux Foundation (and Mozilla I guess) could throw $1.2M of API-rate LLM usage at security. reply komali2 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n This, but for anything Capital-gated. Imagine if all universities could get access to radiotelescope time, atom smasher time, quantum compute research lab access... frontier models on massive datacenters represents a huge new category of captured commons. reply nurumaik 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Tbh I feel less safe in digital world after reading this article. Would be cool to see \"And then we made a formal proof that there's no more vulnerabilities\" someday reply evan_a_a 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Everything is broken, it's just a matter of how bad. Just like it is impossible to write software that is free of bugs, it is impossible to write software that is free of vulnerabilities. Such is the nature of the field. The good news is we have well established tools in security to mitigate the impact of any one vulnerability. reply getcrunk 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > For the near and medium term, having access to a low-privileged account on an operating system, that isn’t up-to-date on its latest available patches should be considered functionally equivalent to root access for a well-resourced threat actor. *buries head in sand* *cries* reply kccqzy 11 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n LPEs are frequent enough that people no longer use low-privilege user accounts to sandbox potentially malicious software. They are only used to control access for well-behaved users without any intent to subvert the system, not attackers. The difference here reminds me of the distinction between the regular Paxos algorithm and the Byzantine Paxos algorithm. reply dbdr 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > people no longer use low-privilege user accounts to sandbox potentially malicious software Is it the same for containers and VMs? If not, what makes them less vulnerable? reply rubinlinux 12 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This has been true for DECADES. reply basilikum 12 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Regular LPEs are not novel. The frequency has just increased a lot. reply bakugo 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Some distros even include LPE as a feature ( cough Ubuntu Server) reply asdff 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Id count windows and macos in there because you just need an admin pass and you have full rights. Average users might reuse that password everywhere too, plus no password requirements, no expiration. So many guides online for various stuff have users typing sudo or run as administrator too, so they don’t look at it with a side eye either if the attack went in via social engineering instead of brute force or using a leaked pass associated with the user. reply keyle 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n While we were developing the pipeline, in late July 2026, GPT‑5.6 Sol found and exploited 166 unique LPE vulnerabilities for an end-to-end $513,690 cost in API credits.\n\n In the second scan, in mid-October 2026, the newer cyber model found and exploited 948 unique LPE vulnerabilities for an end-to-end $1,213,430 cost in API credits. For both campaigns, we scanned the latest Linux kernel master branch at the time.\n \nboy I really wonder though, how many of these 948 LPE are nearly impossible to execute though. reply bzmrgonz 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wonder what Sidero Labs has to say about this article. My hopes are pegged on their efforts. I have my wagon ready to hitch on their horses. They are not a one pony show anymore guys, Talos OS can be standalone server OS, a hypervisor server or cluster, and their flagship of k8s node or cluster. We get all that in an immutable gitops ready OS. I'm practically sold on their offering. reply shepherdjerred 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I love Talos. I've used it for years for a two-node k8s cluster. It just works. reply dmos62 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I love how we're finally putting numbers to our technical debt. reply rishabhd 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Curious question - if any organization sponsored a 1.2mn USD program to find vulnerabilities in Linux, will they find these many vulnerabilities? Or use a professional software like Checkmarx or Veracode to do it with 1.2mn USD in license + human effort. Is it a funding + will problem or is it an AI problem? reply asdff 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I’d think they’d go a long way. You have to remember many jailbreaks in major hardened systems over the decades have come from gifted eastern european teenagers. Imagine giving this talent actual resources and a mandate to do this full time. reply breisa 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Crazy times. These corporations and their governments have access to literal zero-day vending machines. Put $50 in - get another one.\nI mean, yeah, it was clear that certain agencies had heaps of useful exploits at hand for quite some time now. But AI really commoditizes these exploits. If there's source code or even just a binary available, they can hack it. And even if your code is robust and secure, they just pull the lever on the vending machine again and come back with some zero-day for your OS's networking stack. reply ksec 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wonder if they could run it on OpenBSD and FreeBSD as well. reply tiffanyh 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Anthropic ran Mythos against OpenBSD and only had 1 reported vulnerability. https://www.anthropic.com/glasswing reply asdff 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Is this because of security or poor representation in training set? Then again that might not matter, same outcome. reply IcyWindows 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I think this is one area where Windows has an advantage. ACLs, Appcontainers with capabilities, etc have been something in the OS for more than a decade. reply jasonjayr 11 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n There are a lot of LPE's in Windows too. Remember that it started out as a single user operating system, it took a long time to get software on board to not always running as admin. reply easton 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Was NT ever single user? I thought they built it with network/multiuser in mind. (I agree that maybe applications could’ve been built for 3.1/9x and expect a single user, but the os expects multiple). reply perching_aix 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Considering the amount of crazy shit even just user access allows you to do, by design, an LPE is not even really necessary. reply viraptor 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Linux has effectively same options and more via LSM. I'm not sure why ACL is mentioned here since it exists on Linux too. What advantage do you mean specifically? reply matt3210 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n cc <<< 'Rewrite the kernel, all libs and all GNU in rust so we can avoid the unsafe keyword. Make only one mistake and document it in mistake.md' reply sean_pedersen 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n How many of these would have been prevented by using safe Rust? reply clvx 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n and yet thousands of iot devices will not get these updates. This is gonna be fun. reply tarpitt 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Oh no, they will not be secured against their own users :( reply hoppp 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Well at least they are doing public service while hacking the planet. reply snvzz 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n And so many more are hidden in there, in those MLoCs. The only way forward is a better system architecture, as enabled by the seL4[0] microkernel. Any solution involving keeping Linux or the monolithic kernel is just stopgap or outright delusional. 0. https://sel4.systems reply tamimio 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n These are becoming more and more marketing materials than actual findings, because OAI probably hit the wall in making their models smarter (just like how google try to specialize their models in legal or whatever), but they need to remain relevant in the news. Sol 6.1 sucked so bad in reasoning that either it’s actually a bad model or users behind the scenes get degraded outcome while using all the resources for these marketing stunts. Stop, it’s embarrassing, make better models or be gone, at least we will get cheaper RAM to advance the development of open weights models. reply numlock86 5 hours ago | prev [–] \n Plot twist: They look for genuine exploit paths to fix as a cover up, while introducing and potentially selling genuine new backdoors that humans or other models are unlikely to find. Edit: Or maybe the AI does it just for its own interest. Just kidding (or am I?), but this is pretty cool. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
 "page:https://huggingface.co/ricursiveAI/Ripple-65B-Preview/raw/main/README.md": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T12:48:57.152Z",
  "bytes": 12376,
  "text": "---\nlicense: apache-2.0\n---\n# Ripple-65B Preview\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n<picture>\n  <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/rSaJGoPP1Saln5kpScZNGCw.png\" width=\"30%\">\n  <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/3rFANSmoZuSBhjRwxZolyEU5guo.png\" width=\"30%\">\n  <img alt=\"Ricursive Logo\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/3rFANSmoZuSBhjRwxZolyEU5guo.png\" width=\"30%\">\n</picture>\n</div>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/ricursiveAI/Ripple-65B-Preview\">BF16</a> |\n  <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/ricursiveAI/Ripple-65B-Preview-NVFP4\">NVFP4</a> |\n  <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/ricursiveAI/Ripple-65B-Base-Preview\">Base Model</a> |\n  <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/ricursiveAI/Ripple-65B-Preview-DFlash2\">DFlash2</a> |\n  <a href=\"https://www.ricursive.com/ripple\">Launch Blog</a> | \n  License: <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/ricursiveAI/Ripple-65B-Preview/blob/main/LICENSE\">Apache 2.0</a>\n</p>\n\nRicursive Intelligence introduces **Ripple-65B Preview**, a 65B parameter dense Transformer model pretrained on 12 trillion tokens with support for up to 1M context length. \nRipple-65B Preview delivers strong results on agentic coding, scoring **80.52 on TerminalBench 2.1** and\nsurpassing significantly-larger US open-weight models such as Reflection Beam 501B (**80.1**), Meta Muse Spark Open 816B (**76.4**), \nThinking Machines Inkling 975B (**63.8**), and NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra 550B (**56.4**). \n\nOur Preview release includes an instruction-tuned model in BF16 and NVFP4 numerics, and a BF16 base model. **This repo contains the BF16 instruction-tuned model.**\n\nStarting from an initial Olmo 3 baseline, we iteratively improved all aspects of the training recipe including pretraining scaling laws, model architecture, data, and posttraining stack.\nOur final Ripple v1 model ladder improves Compute Efficiency (CE) on code by **16x** relative to the Olmo 3 ladder, with the advantage further increasing with scale up to \n**55x** for the full hero run. \n\nRipple-65B was codesigned to be trained and served efficiently on NVIDIA hardware.\nFor our first model, a key objective was to make design decisions which reduce pretraining and posttraining\ninfrastructure complexity while still enabling strong reasoning and agentic performance.\nTherefore, we chose to train a modernized dense Transformer model rather than use Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) like other labs.\nThis decision quickly paid off, and we were able to train a strong model from scratch with a small team that scaled from 1 to 5 people during development, \nwhile maintaining solid MFU (40%) throughout pretraining and posttraining with simple sharding, no custom kernels, and no quantization (BF16).\n\nRipple-65B uses a **thin-and-deep** model aspect ratio, with a narrow d_model (5120) and 80 total layers.\nWe switch from the Olmo 3 llama-like architecture to a Gemma 3N-like architecture to improve training stability at scale. \nTo improve reasoning performance, we **overprovision the number of attention heads** while increasing the SWA-to-global layer ratio \nand reducing SWA window size to compensate for the increased cost.\nIn addition, we added FFN activation clipping, gated attention, global NoPE, and value residuals.\n\nThe end result is a model that is especially capable at reasoning and agentic tasks given its model size, matching or exceeding other \nUS open-weight models with more than 10x the number of total parameters.\nAt only 65B total parameters, the model can fit on consumer hardware such as the NVIDIA DGX Spark. \nTo encourage local model development, we provide an NVFP4 checkpoint and colab to help you get started. \nThe model is also fast at token generation when running on datacenter GPUs, reaching 250 tok/s on 2xB200 batch=8 prior to quantization or custom kernels. \n\n**Pre-training.** Ripple-65B Preview is pretrained from scratch on our 12T text-only predominantly-English and code corpus at 8K sequence length. \nRipple-65B Base Preview is a **pretrain-only checkpoint** without any midtraining or synthetic QA data.\n\n**Post-training.** We use a standard post-training recipe consisting of midtraining, long context, SFT, long context SFT, and mixed domain RL. \nOur Preview release has gone through relatively light posttraining, with a focus on agentic coding.\n\nWe show a brief overview of each stage below:\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n| Stage | Tokens | Step | Seqlen | Batch Size | \n| ----- | ------ | ------ | ---- | ----- |\n| Pretrain | 12T | 877,500 | 8K | 1408 -> 3072 |\n| Midtrain | 200B | 8,000 | 64K | 384 |\n| Long Context | 6.5B | 389 | 2M | 8 |\n| SFT | 183B | 7,274 | 128K | 192 |\n| LC SFT | 13.5B | 802 | 2M | 8 | \n| RL  | N/A  | 700 | 128K | 2048 |\n\n</div>\n\n## Instruct Model Benchmarks\n\nWe compare Ripple-65B Preview against currently-available US open-weight frontier models.\nAll models are tested at full BF16 precision with maximum effort levels.\n\nEach lab typically only provides a limited subset of available benchmarks.\nOur table below shows official model card scores where available, and we use our own in-house eval flow for missing evals (italized).\nFor SWE-bench Pro v2.0 Hard and DeepSWE 1.1, we use Terminus 2 for Ripple-65B Preview.\nIn other cases where the harness is not specified, we use OpenCode.\nA higher number is better for all benchmarks.\n\nReplicating benchmark scores has been challenging, particularly for modern agentic evals, due to the number of factors which can cause \neval scores to shift. \nFor example, we found that Terminal-Bench 2.1 scores were universally lower (with the exception of Muse Spark Open) \nwhen using the official Terminus 2 harness at 128K sequence length with summarization.\nTherefore, our results represent a best-effort attempt at a fair comparison.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n  | Benchmark | Ripple-65B<br>Preview | Nemotron 3<br>Ultra 550B | Inkling<br>975B | Muse Spark<br>Open 816B | Reflection<br>Beam 501B |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | **Model** | | | | | |\n  | Architecture | Dense | MoE | MoE | MoE | MoE |\n  | Total params | 65B | 550B | **975B** | 816B | 501B |\n  | Active params | **65B** | 55B | 41B | 42B | 23B |\n  | Pre-training tokens | 12T | 20T | **45T** | N/A | 23.8T |\n  | **Knowledge** | | | | | |\n  | MMLU | 89.6 | *92.0* | *91.3* | ***92.6*** | *92.4* |\n  | MMLU-Pro | 84.2 | 86.8 | *87.3* | ***87.9*** | *76.5* |\n  | GPQA Diamond | 85.2 | 87.0 | 87.2 | 89.0 | **90.6** |\n  | **Math** | | | | | |\n  | AIME 2024 | 97.1 | *94.2* | *97.5* | ***97.9*** | *96.7* | \n  | AIME 2025 | 98.3 | *94.2* | *91.7* | ***100.0*** | *99.6* |\n  | AIME 2026 | 95.0 | *95.4* | 97.1 | ***98.3*** | 97.7 |\n  | HMMT Feb 2026 | 90.5 | *80.3* | 86.3 | ***93.9*** | ***93.9*** | \n  | **Coding & Agentic** | | | | | |\n  | Terminal-Bench 2.1 (self-reported) | **80.5** | 56.4 | 63.8 | 76.4 | 80.1 |\n  | Terminal-Bench 2.1 (Terminus 2) | **80.5** | *55.1* | *56.2* | *78.7* | *70.8* |\n  | SWE-bench Verified | 76.5 | 71.9 | 77.6 | *78.7* | **81.6** |\n  | SWE-bench Pro v1.0 | 61.42 | *46.9* | 54.3 | *58.2* | **66.1** |\n  | SWE-bench Pro v2.0 Hard | **79.1** | *30.7* | *2.0* | *52.3* | 76.5 |\n  | DeepSWE 1.1 | 44.3 | *0.2* | *1.8* | **60.0** | 43.3 |\n  | SciCode (Inspect AI) | 39.4 | 44.6 | 46.1 | **58.5** | 49.2 | \n  | τ³-bench banking | 31.27 | 22.6 | 23.7 | *14.4* | **36.1** |\n  | AutomationBench (Public) | 36.4 | *8.4* | *14.6* | *42.4* | 39.3 | \n  | MCP Atlas | 71.67 | *62.2* | 76.0 | **89.2** | 80.8 |\n  | **Safety** | | | | | |\n  | FORTRESS Adversarial | 83.8 | *79.3* | 78.0 | **94.2** | *92.1* |\n  | FORTRESS Benign | 95.5 | *89.1* | **95.9** | *82.6* | *86.4* |\n  | StrongREJECT | 97.5 | *98.2* | **98.6** | *94.7* | *95.8* |\n  | **Instruction Following**  | | | | | |\n  | IFBench | 79.2 | **81.7** | 79.8 | *78.6* | 79.6 |\n  | **Long Context**  | | | | | |\n  | AA-LCR | 75.0 | 65.4 | *78.3* | ***80.7*** | 78.2 |\n\n</div>\n\n\n## Base Model Benchmarks\n\nWe show traditional pretraining evals comparing Ripple-65B Base Preview against a selection of open-weight base models. Although we would have liked to \ncompare against the same set of models throughout the model card, many companies are now choosing to only open-source their instruction-tuned models.\n\nWith the rise of the importance of posttraining, many modern LLM recipes now blend posttraining data earlier into the training process, including during pretraining.\nFor our first model, we kept our pretraining data clean with minimal synthetic data, focusing on high quality natural data rather than aggressively targeting evals.\nWe instead added significantly more synthetic data during midtraining, which allowed us to quickly iterate on our synthetic data recipe without blocking pretraining.\n\nRipple-65B Base Preview is a pretrain-only checkpoint. The base model overall beats Poolside Laguna M.1,\nand is comparable with DeepSeek V3.1 and V4 Flash on reasoning, math, and code.\nNemotron 3 Ultra dominates on traditional pretraining evals, likely due to their heavy use of [task-seeded synthetic data](https://huggingface.co/blog/nvidia/task-seeded-sdg).\nHowever, its pretraining eval performance did not translate to modern agentic tasks, likely due to their synthetic data being out of distribution relative to these tasks.\n\nWe report official model results where available.\nFor our own model and for missing evals, we use our internal fork of [Olmes](https://github.com/allenai/olmes).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n  | Benchmark (Metric) | # Shots | Ripple-65B<br>Preview | Laguna<br>M.1 225B | DeepSeek<br>V3.1 671B | DeepSeek<br>V4 Flash 284B | Nemotron 3<br>Ultra 550B |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | **Model** | | | | | | |\n  | Architecture | — | Dense | MoE | MoE | MoE | MoE |\n  | Total params | — | 65B | 225B | **671B** | 284B | 550B |\n  | Active params | — | **65B** | 23B | 37B | 13B | 55B |\n  | Training tokens (T) | — | 12T | 30T+ | 15.6T | **32T+** | 20T |\n  | **World Knowledge** | | | | | | |\n  | MMLU (MC acc) | 5-shot | *83.7* | *76.6* | *88.3* | 88.7 | **89.1** |\n  | MMLU-Pro (CoT EM) | 5-shot | *61.5* | *52.2* | *57.8* | 68.3 | **79.1** |\n  | AGIEval-En (acc) | 3-5-shot | *74.4* | *66.2* | *81.9* | **83.9** | 78.7 |\n  | GPQA main (CoT EM) | 5-shot | *37.7* | *31.7* | *30.8* | *44.0* | ***49.6*** |\n  | GPQA-Diamond (CoT EM) | 5-shot | *38.4* | *27.8* | *37.4* | *44.4* | ***53.0*** |\n  | C-Eval (MC acc) | 5-shot | *69.6* | *59.3* | *90.7* | **92.1** | *82.2* |\n  | SuperGPQA (MC acc) | 5-shot | *34.2* | *24.5* | *43.1* | 46.5 | ***52.9*** |\n  | SimpleQA-Verified (EM) | 25-shot | *10.1* | *10.8* | *17.3* | **30.1** | *27.1* |\n  | **Language & Reasoning** | | | | | | | \n  | BBH (CoT EM) | 3-shot | *85.6* | *79.6* | *87.2* | 86.9 | ***88.0*** | \n  | BBEH (EM) | 1-shot | *17.9* | *14.2* | *18.4* | **25.4** | *21.2* |\n  | DROP (F1) | 1-shot | *77.8* | *66.8* | *81.2* | **88.6** | *87.6* |\n  | HellaSwag (acc_norm) | 0-shot | *84.6* | *86.2* | *86.8* | 85.7 | ***89.1*** |\n  | HellaSwag (acc_norm) | 10-shot | *87.2* | *88.1* | *89.4* | *88.8* | **90.5** |\n  | ARC-Challenge (MC acc) | 25-shot | *95.7* | *90.8* | *95.1* | *95.8* | **97.4** |\n  | OpenBookQA (acc_norm) | 0-shot | *47.4* | *48.6* | ***50.4*** | *48.0* | 48.6 |\n  | PIQA (acc_norm) | 0-shot | *83.6* | *84.8* | ***85.2*** | *84.7* | 83.8 |\n  | WinoGrande (acc) | 5-shot | ***83.4*** | *83.2* | *83.2* | *81.4* | 79.3 |\n  | **Code & Math** | | | | | | |\n  | HumanEval (pass@1, n=20, T=0.2) | 0-shot | *70.9* | *83.4* | *69.8* | 69.5 | **83.8** |\n  | MBPP Sanitized (pass@1, n=32, T=0.2) | 3-shot | *81.5* | *81.1* | *75.2* | *74.6* | **86.0** |\n  | BigCodeBench (pass@1, n=5, T=0.8) | 3-shot | *49.2* | *50.5* | ***59.9*** | 56.8 | *55.6* |\n  | GSM8K (CoT EM) | 8-shot | *88.5* | *80.4* | *79.6* | **90.8** | 88.1 |\n  | GSM1K (CoT EM) | 8-shot | *88.4* | *79.7* | *81.8* | *83.2* | ***90.2*** |\n  | MATH (flex EM) | 4-shot | *59.1* | *52.5* | *59.7* | 57.4 | **82.0** |\n  | MGSM (CoT EM, 11-lang avg) | 8-shot | *78.2* | *65.4* | *81.7* | 85.7 | **87.7** |\n\n\n</div>\n\n## Citation\n\n```bibtex\n@misc{ricursive2026ripple65b,\n      title={Ripple-65B: Pushing the Frontier of Local Intelligence},\n      author={Ricursive Intelligence},\n      year={2026},\n}\n```\n\n## Contact\n\nFor questions and comments, please contact us at [info@ricursive.com](mailto:info@ricursive.com). To learn more, please visit our [website](https://www.ricursive.com)."
