 "page:https://huggingface.co/bubskee/qwen3.5-4b-r-jpp-lenses/raw/main/README.md": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T12:50:24.131Z",
  "bytes": 11077,
  "text": "---\nlicense: apache-2.0\nbase_model: Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B\nlibrary_name: pytorch\ntags:\n- interpretability\n- workspace-lens\n- r-lens\n- jpp-lens\n- qwen3.5\n- experimental\n---\n\n# R and J++ lenses for Qwen3.5-4B\n\nR and J++ workspace lenses for the text decoder of **Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B**, with a standalone reader, frozen evaluation results, and fitting provenance. Released by [bubskee](https://huggingface.co/bubskee).\n\nIf you're already poking at neurons in this model, I'd love you to look through these lenses and tell me what you see. Compare their readouts with top-activating examples and with what interventions actually change. Disagreements and boring failures are useful too. ^^\n\nThe release contains the original pooled R map and the original signed J++ map. Both have one FP32 transport matrix per source layer. J++ improves concept recovery on the heldout tasks below, with much of its multilingual gain coming from relations and input concepts. These readouts are hypotheses to investigate; they are not causal labels for neurons.\n\n## Start with a prompt\n\nPython 3.12 was used. The reader supports CUDA, Apple Silicon MPS, and CPU; the packaged smoke test used MPS. It loads the native BF16 text model, FP32 transport maps, and an FP32 readout head. The base model is downloaded separately. Quantized base-model compatibility has not been established.\n\n```bash\nhf download bubskee/qwen3.5-4b-r-jpp-lenses --local-dir qwen35-lenses\npip install -r qwen35-lenses/requirements.txt\npython qwen35-lenses/read_lenses.py --release qwen35-lenses \\\n  --prompt 'Fact: The number of legs on the animal that spins webs is' \\\n  --layers 8 16 24 30\n```\n\nThis prints logit, R, and J++ top-ten readouts. Add `--kind jpp` to select one lens or `--raw` to rank the full vocabulary. Default ranking uses the saved upstream filter for tokens without letters or digits, with evaluation candidate tokens spared. Prompts are raw completion prefixes, matching the evaluation format; the reader does not add a chat template. Default position is the final token, with a 512-token prompt cap. `--position` selects another token position.\n\n## Inspect a neuron write direction\n\n```bash\npython qwen35-lenses/read_lenses.py --release qwen35-lenses \\\n  --neuron 1234 --layers 20\n```\n\nHere, neuron 1234 means the dense MLP channel after gating and before `down_proj` in decoder block 20. The reader projects the corresponding column of `down_proj.weight` through each lens and prints both signs. This is vocabulary alignment of an **isolated residual-write direction**. An actual context also supplies the neuron's activation, other neurons' contributions, and the surrounding residual stream. Check whether the proposed interpretation survives those changes and causal interventions.\n\nFor an activation you've already captured, pass a safetensors file containing the raw finite nonzero vector `activation`, shape `[2560]`:\n\n```bash\npython qwen35-lenses/read_lenses.py --release qwen35-lenses \\\n  --vector activation.safetensors --layers 20\n```\n\nThe Python interface is `WorkspaceLenses(release, device=\"auto\")`, with `capture`, `transport`, `logits`, `read`, and `neuron_direction` methods in `read_lenses.py`. No local training repository or upstream package is needed for inference. The `.pt` maps also use the upstream workspace-lens format and are loaded with `torch.load(weights_only=True)`.\n\n## Model and layer contract\n\n- Base: `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B`, revision `851bf6e806efd8d0a36b00ddf55e13ccb7b8cd0a`.\n- Source layers: zero-based decoder block outputs 0 through 30, width 2560.\n- Transport target: output of block 31, **before final RMSNorm**.\n- Readout: FP32 final RMSNorm, including Qwen3.5's `1 + weight` offset, followed by the FP32 native tied LM head.\n- Inputs: raw residuals, without whitening or input normalization.\n\nThe model has 32 blocks: 24 Gated DeltaNet and 8 full-attention blocks, with dense MLPs. The eight “experts” used during J++ fitting are clusters of source activations, not native MoE experts. At inference, both R and J++ use one fixed linear map per layer.\n\n## What the lenses recover\n\nOriginal A40 evaluation: 265 heldout items across five public upstream tasks, disjoint from 64 calibration items. Rank is the best over 31 source layers for each representable annotated concept. Scores average concepts within prompts, prompts within tasks, and then tasks equally for the macro. These are concept-readout scores, not generated-answer accuracy.\n\n| Metric | Logit lens | R | J++ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Macro top-1, full vocabulary | 24.7% | 23.2% | 35.7% |\n| Macro top-10, full vocabulary | 39.0% | 44.1% | 57.2% |\n| Macro top-1, filtered | 24.8% | 27.9% | 38.0% |\n| Macro top-10, filtered | 39.3% | 49.0% | 59.4% |\n| Multilingual top-1, filtered | 19.2% | 30.2% | 40.7% |\n| Multilingual top-10, filtered | 33.3% | 56.2% | 76.2% |\n\nThe multilingual task reads semantic concepts from non-English prompts, often through English token labels. On its 43 heldout prompts, filtered top-1 breaks down as follows:\n\n| Concept role | R | J++ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Language identifier | 1/42 | 3/42 |\n| Relation or operation | 7/43 | 15/43 |\n| Input concept | 6/43 | 12/43 |\n| Answer concept | 38/43 | 40/43 |\n\nFourteen of the eighteen additional concept hits are relations or inputs. Searching depth matters: at the same fixed block 27, multilingual filtered top-1 is 18.6% for R and 19.8% for J++, compared with 30.2% and 40.7% when searching all 31 layers.\n\nThere are substantial failures. Both lenses score zero poetry top-1; J++ association top-1 is 4.9%. Raw typo top-1 is 36.4% for R, 68.8% for logit lens, and 81.8% for J++. R is not uniformly better than the direct logit readout.\n\nFull ranks, per-layer results, split membership, and summaries are in `evaluation/`. The figures show the depth and concept-role breakdowns.\n\n## What is earning J++ its gains\n\nA separate local ablation kept the original calibration/test split and readout fixed. Single-expert selection used calibration only. Positive-only and signed mixtures used the same top-10 objective, three starts, and 200 Adam steps per start; all weights were sealed before heldout inference. These are diagnostic results on the already-inspected public test set.\n\n| Local control | Macro top-10, filtered | Multilingual top-1, filtered | Multilingual top-10, filtered |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| R | 48.9% | 30.2% | 55.6% |\n| Uniform experts | 46.9% | 29.1% | 53.3% |\n| Calibration-selected single expert | 55.4% | 39.5% | 69.6% |\n| Positive-only fitted mixture | 55.4% | 36.6% | 69.0% |\n| Signed matched refit | 58.8% | 38.4% | 73.1% |\n| Original J++ replayed locally | 59.1% | 40.1% | 75.0% |\n\nSelection recovers much of the observed top-1 gain; equal averaging does not. Signed mixing adds recall under this matched search budget. The single-expert control also loses three multilingual prompts that R retained, while original J++ loses none. Its language-identifier recovery is stronger, but its answer-concept recovery is weaker. The readers preserve complementary content.\n\nLocal MPS forwards are not bit-identical to CUDA. The original J++ replay reaches 40.1% multilingual filtered top-1, compared with 40.7% on A40. Every control in the local table shares the same local activations. `analysis/` contains the full ablation report, prompt-level retention, paired bootstrap intervals, protocol, and integrity checks. The diagnostic control weights are not promoted as replacement releases.\n\n## Fitting and provenance\n\nBoth lenses share LRP-backward Jacobian statistics from 64 neutral WikiText sequences, capped at 128 tokens, with 7104 retained source positions. J++ uses eight activation clusters per layer, PCA 64 routing, a minimum support of 50 positions, and pooled-R fallback for under-supported experts. Three early expert slots fall back to R.\n\nR is exported from FP32 pooled statistics. J++ calibrates a signed mixture of the persisted FP16 expert means, then exports the combined maps in FP32. Calibration uses the canonical upstream per-layer pair-macro top-10 objective and default three-start search. The local SiLU patch preserves stock BF16 forward values while retaining the intended R-Lens backward rule.\n\nMethod: [J++ Lens, Ayonrinde and Lindsey](https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/nc9dHfcB22JdMzGbr/j-lens-jacobian-filtering-enables-more-faithful-workspace), using [the upstream implementation](https://github.com/safety-research/jpp_lens) at revision `d7efc0de372e20589e899188c0cfe8434acc63ec`. Original workspace lenses: [Gurnee et al.](https://transformer-circuits.pub/2026/workspace/index.html). R-Lens implementation: [Blank and Bhatia](https://huggingface.co/camilablank/workspace-lenses).\n\n`provenance/` records the recipe, expert coefficients/support, model integrity audit, and corpus hashes. `training/value_exact_rlens.py` contains the forward-preservation patch. Raw training text, raw activations, expert banks, optimizer states, and base-model weights are not bundled. The original dataset revision was not independently pinned before download; saved corpus/per-prompt hashes identify the data used. `SHA256SUMS` covers the release payload. See `LICENSE` and `NOTICE` for attribution.\n\n## Read longer descriptions with the O-lens\n\nThe related [Qwen3.5-4B O-lens release](https://huggingface.co/bubskee/qwen3.5-4b-o-lenses) provides experimental activation-to-text adapters at **L26**, with its own reader and contracts. R/J++ return ranked vocabulary tokens; the O-lens generates longer descriptions of an activation.\n\nTo save a contextual activation in the format its reader expects:\n\n```bash\npython qwen35-lenses/read_lenses.py --release qwen35-lenses \\\n  --prompt 'Fact: The number of legs on the animal that spins webs is' \\\n  --layers 26 --save-activation activation.safetensors\n```\n\nThen follow the O-lens repository's CUDA reader instructions and exact L26 contract. Its historical base revision is recorded as unspecified, so this is an exploratory cross-reader comparison rather than a claim of verified historical base identity. Isolated neuron directions are unvalidated O-lens inputs. At this release, the linked public O-lens repository contains L26; L30 is still being completed separately.\n\n## Limits and useful feedback\n\nThe public test is small and uses single-token concept surfaces. The competence gate accepts leading answer subwords; it does not establish full-answer correctness. Four European languages account for 34/43 multilingual prompts, with most remaining languages represented once. Best-over-layers recovery is more permissive than reading one predetermined layer. No causal intervention study accompanies this 4B release.\n\nI'd particularly like examples where a readout disagrees with interventions, where R and J++ disagree, or where a language/concept repeatedly fails. Open a [HF discussion](https://huggingface.co/bubskee/qwen3.5-4b-r-jpp-lenses/discussions) with the prompt, layer, token position, neuron/channel index if relevant, raw/filtered setting, and what you tested. Please keep private prompts out of public reports.\n"
 },
