  "text": " OpenAI says GPT-6 Cyber is better at finding vulnerabilities without going out of bounds | The Verge Skip to main content The homepage Tech Reviews Science Entertainment AI Policy The homepage Navigation Drawer Login / Sign Up close Search Light System Dark Tech Amazon Apple Facebook Google Microsoft Samsung Business See all tech Reviews Smart Home Reviews Phone Reviews Tablet Reviews Headphone Reviews See all reviews Science Space Energy Environment Health See all science Entertainment TV Shows Movies Audio See all entertainment AI OpenAI Anthropic See all AI Policy Antitrust Politics Law Security See all policy Gadgets Laptops Phones TVs Headphones Speakers Wearables See all gadgets Verge Shopping Buying Guides Deals Gift Guides See all shopping Gaming Xbox PlayStation Nintendo See all gaming Streaming Disney HBO Netflix YouTube Creators See all streaming Transportation Electric Cars Autonomous Cars Ride-sharing Scooters See all transportation Features Verge Video TikTok YouTube Instagram Podcasts Decoder The Vergecast Version History Newsletters Archives Store Verge Product Updates Subscribe OpenAI says its new cybersecurity model is better at not hacking other companies Comments Drawer Comments Loading comments Getting the conversation ready... AI AI Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All AI News News Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All News OpenAI OpenAI Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All OpenAI OpenAI says its new cybersecurity model is better at not hacking other companies Classified as ‘Critical,’ GPT-6 Cyber outperforms GPT-6 Astra on cyber-related benchmarks. Classified as ‘Critical,’ GPT-6 Cyber outperforms GPT-6 Astra on cyber-related benchmarks. by Emma Roth Emma Roth News Writer Posts from this author will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All by Emma Roth Nov 5, 2026, 5:00 PM UTC Image: The Verge Emma Roth Emma Roth Posts from this author will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All by Emma Roth is a news writer who covers the streaming wars, consumer tech, crypto, social media, and much more. Previously, she was a writer and editor at MUO. OpenAI is launching GPT-6 Cyber, the latest version of its cybersecurity AI model, which it says is better at finding vulnerabilities and less likely to go rogue and hack other platforms in the process. OpenAI says that during testing, GPT-6 Cyber went out of bounds less than 1 percent of the time, down from around 5 percent with GPT-5.6 Cyber. The evaluation, called ExploitGym HoneyPot, is modeled after the Hugging Face Incident, in which OpenAI’s AI agents breached the open-source AI community to find answers to a problem they were tasked with solving. “With the 6 Cyber model, we were able to make huge improvements on this evaluation,” Fouad Matin, the head of OpenAI’s cyber preparedness and safety team, said during a press briefing. “It’s over 99 percent of the time doing what you ask.” But evaluation awareness , or when an AI system recognizes that it’s being tested and changes its behavior in response, remains a concern. “Evaluation awareness is super top of mind for us,” Matin said. “Often when we do see evaluation awareness, you’ll see it in the model’s chain of thought.” Matin added that OpenAI is trying to prioritize production-like evaluations, where the model is interacting with real codebases with a real objective to lessen the risk of awareness. Following a wave of AI cybersecurity incidents, OpenAI has raced to strengthen its safeguards and lock down its most powerful models. The company briefly paused training of its frontier AI models, and it now restricts their access to the internet . GPT-6 Cyber is classified as Critical in cybersecurity on OpenAI’s Preparedness Framework , which indicates that it “can identify and develop functional zero-day exploits of all severity levels in many hardened real-world critical systems without human intervention.” GPT-5.6 Cyber was previously classified as High under the framework. GPT-6 Cyber outperforms GPT-6 Astra and GPT-5.6 Sol on ExploitGym. Image: OpenAI GPT-6 Cyber outperforms GPT-6 Astra, OpenAI’s flagship Critical-level model , on most cyber-related tasks. In ExploitGym, an AI benchmark that measures whether an AI model can exploit vulnerabilities, GPT-6 Cyber scored 48.5 percent, about 16 points higher than GPT-5.6 Cyber’s 32.1 percent and a few points higher than GPT-6 Astra’s 42.4 percent score. The model helped find 81 previously unknown vulnerabilities in Firefox, and it played a role in hardening OpenAI’s own infrastructure for training AI models, OpenAI says. Additionally, GPT-6 Cyber comes with stronger safeguards “to detect and disrupt misuse” while better adhering to user instructions. OpenAI says it’s using new checks to block “high-risk activity,” including attacks on critical infrastructure like power plants and water treatment facilities. Related Inside the suddenly explosive world of AI safety OpenAI now trains its frontier AI models with no internet access GPT-6 Cyber is rolling out to select members of OpenAI’s Daybreak Red program, which is available to companies and organizations using its models for “authorized defensive cybersecurity work.” It’s expanding GPT-6 Cyber to all Daybreak Red customers in the coming weeks. Follow topics and authors from this story to see more like this in your personalized homepage feed and to receive email updates. Emma Roth Emma Roth News Writer Posts from this author will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All by Emma Roth AI AI Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All AI News News Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your ho