  "text": " Discord is ditching Electron and its new app uses less RAM | The Verge Skip to main content The homepage Tech Reviews Science Entertainment AI Policy The homepage Navigation Drawer Login / Sign Up close Search Light System Dark Tech Amazon Apple Facebook Google Microsoft Samsung Business See all tech Reviews Smart Home Reviews Phone Reviews Tablet Reviews Headphone Reviews See all reviews Science Space Energy Environment Health See all science Entertainment TV Shows Movies Audio See all entertainment AI OpenAI Anthropic See all AI Policy Antitrust Politics Law Security See all policy Gadgets Laptops Phones TVs Headphones Speakers Wearables See all gadgets Verge Shopping Buying Guides Deals Gift Guides See all shopping Gaming Xbox PlayStation Nintendo See all gaming Streaming Disney HBO Netflix YouTube Creators See all streaming Transportation Electric Cars Autonomous Cars Ride-sharing Scooters See all transportation Features Verge Video TikTok YouTube Instagram Podcasts Decoder The Vergecast Version History Newsletters Archives Store Verge Product Updates Subscribe Discord is ditching Electron and its new app uses less RAM Comments Drawer Comments Loading comments Getting the conversation ready... News News Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All News Gaming Gaming Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All Gaming Tech Tech Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All Tech Discord is ditching Electron and its new app uses less RAM Discord is moving its apps fully over to the Rust programming language. Discord is moving its apps fully over to the Rust programming language. by Tom Warren Tom Warren Senior Correspondent Posts from this author will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All by Tom Warren Nov 5, 2026, 11:39 AM UTC Illustration by Alex Castro / The Verge Tom Warren Tom Warren Posts from this author will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All by Tom Warren is a senior correspondent and author of Notepad , who has been covering all things Microsoft, PC, and tech for over 20 years. Discord is finally giving up on Electron, the cross-platform framework that lets developers build desktop apps using web technologies. Electron web apps are notoriously heavy on system resources, and Discord is now moving its desktop app fully to Rust — a modern programming language designed for speed and memory safety. “We’ve been early adopters of Rust since 2018 and a lot of our infra, voice tech, and even some of the current client is already Rust, the holy grail was getting the whole client there,” explains Discord chief technology officer Stanislav Vishnevskiy in a post on X . “That means Electron goes away, and so does React Native, mobile keeps native UI but shares app code with desktop via Rust.” Vishnevskiy also shared an image of the Discord Windows client using just 290MB of RAM, which is a clear improvement as you only have to participate in a few servers and RAM usage can easily hit 1GB or more using the current Windows app. It’s not clear when Discord will make the full switch over to Rust, but Vishnevskiy says the company “hope to get it into testing with users soon.” Follow topics and authors from this story to see more like this in your personalized homepage feed and to receive email updates. Tom Warren Tom Warren Senior Correspondent Posts from this author will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All by Tom Warren Creators Creators Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All Creators Gaming Gaming Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest and your homepage feed. Follow See All Gaming News News Posts from this topic will be added to your daily email digest an