  "text": " Discord is ditching Electron and its new app uses less RAM | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login Discord is ditching Electron and its new app uses less RAM ( theverge.com ) 297 points by archb 7 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 245 comments help \n \n the__alchemist 5 hours ago | next [–] \n I had trouble reading more than the opening volley of the article due to popups. Am a Discord user, and rust application programmer: What GUI framework are they using? EGUI? GPUI? Something custom? GPUI from comments? I meant to try GPUI, but backed off after an announcement pending its maintenance would be in question (?) a few months ago, and Zed broadly ending up being a false messiah, for my uses. (Not on the tier of JetBrains IDEs, a bug with a long thread they closed which (still I believe?) causes it to open cached versions of files, then clobber edits made since the cache, e.g. by other programs. I.e. the sort of bug which renders software too dangerous to use. I will not switch to a GUI framework from a group that ignores bugs of that severity reported by many people) reply earthnail 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n They use GPUI. Says so on X. \" (And it's not Tauri, it's GPUI, so good guess everyone.) \" reply sphars 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Which I believe GPUI is the same framework that Zed (the editor)[0] is using [0]: http://zed.dev/ reply ethin 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The fact they're using gpui is... Bad. Is someone trying to make it integrate with native accessibility frameworks? If not (and if they force the native app on us) this transition is going to lock out a ton of people who use assistive technologies. reply tensor 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yes, see: https://github.com/longbridge/gpui-kit . I've been following these rust UI toolkits and things like accessibility have consistently been missing. This is the first time I've seen a UI library that appears complete enough to build a real product with. reply ethin 36 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yeah, that's what worries me. Pretty much every Rust UI library I've seen which isn't wxdragon/Relm/GTK lacks accessibility. And most of them don't even have the primitives for it (given that most of them are abandoned). reply the__alchemist 29 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n This is one angle. I do not see it as a deal breaker in the way you phrase it. I suppose we all have our own. If everyone's had to be satisfied, we'd have no software! I think in the specific case of Discord, I.e. from a big company, I agree they should handle this properly. As a broader standard, I am not as concerned. reply ethin 2 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n This is a dealbreaker for me even if I didn't want it to be. If your application does not either interact with a11y frameworks (UIA, AT-SPI, NSAccessibility, ...) or does not implement an equivalent that communicates with my screen reader, I literally cannot use your application. I could OCR your application, or use AI and have it use your app for me via screenshots, but neither are even remotely comparable to using the native platform accessibility frameworks. Forgeties79 21 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It’s potentially a dealbreaker for millions of people whether they want it to be or not unfortunately. For them accessibility is not optional or can otherwise be taken as a “wait and see” issue. reply anthony-eid 14 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n gpui does have a11y support for accessibility, Zed just hasn't piped it through yet. I'm also pretty sure Discord is using their own fork of gpui with patches to add any missing functionality they needed reply davkan 27 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Could maybe use the browser client as a workaround. reply lsaferite 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Considering the fact that the gpui code only exists as a crate under the zed repo, I'd say you are correct. reply porphyra 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n weird how the website doesn't have a single screenshot on it, considering it's for a GRAPHICAL user interface. You'd think the examples page would have some screenshots, but in fact it's just example code snippets with no indication of what the code would render as. [1] https://gpui.rs/ [2] https://gpui.rs/examples/ reply drcongo 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I've built a couple of desktop apps for myself recently using GPUI-kit [0] and it's been fantastic. I'm not a Rust programmer, so leaned heavily on Opus 5.5 for both, but the end result is two apps that are considerably faster than anything I could pay money for, and tailored to exactly what I need. [0] https://gpui-kit.com reply slashink 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n We're using GPUI for this. reply 1024bits 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n We've been using Iced for Totem [0], our cross-platform collaborative workspace app. Incidentally we took a similar route to what Discord is aiming to do, layering a rust engine with native UI (e.g. Swift on mobile) and it has been going well so far. The one major concern I have with this framework, and with rust UI frameworks in general, is accessibility. That's something you get for free or nearly-free in other ecosystems but seems to require a fair bit of elbow grease here. Still, I would wholeheartedly recommend Iced, with the one caveat that it hasn't yet hit 1.0 and version upgrades could be a little painful, and might bring big changes. [0] https://thinktotem.com reply satvikpendem 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > I had trouble reading more than the opening volley of the article due to popups. Why do people not use adblockers even this late since their introduction? reply hbn 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The Verge is paywalled now but the article really doesn't have much content other than \"they're working on a Rust client and someone showed an early build using 290MB of RAM\" reply kayson 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I'm very interested in (and a total novice in) cross platform GUI application development. Hadn't heard of GPUI, though I had heard of Tauri. I looked at the GPUI front page and saw this mess of almost-CSS method chaining... Surely there's a better way? This seems horrible to me. > div()\n .flex()\n .flex_col()\n .gap_3()\n .bg(rgb(0x505050))\n .size(px(500.0))\n .justify_center()\n .items_center()\n .shadow_lg()\n .border_1()\n .border_color(rgb(0x0000ff))\n .text_xl()\n .text_color(rgb(0xffffff))\n .child(format!(\"Hello, {}!\", &self.text))\n .child(\n div()\n .flex()\n .gap_2()\n .child(div().size_8().bg(gpui::red()))\n .child(div().size_8().bg(gpui::green()))\n .child(div().size_8().bg(gpui::blue()))\n .child(div().size_8().bg(gpui::yellow()))\n .child(div().size_8().bg(gpui::black()))\n .child(div().size_8().bg(gpui::white())),\n ) reply ogoffart 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n IMHO Rust isn't a great language for describing UIs because it is too explicit in places that doesn't need to be for coding a UI.\nWhich is why in Slint (another Rust UI framework) we opted for a DSL. The application logic stays in Rust, but the UI is described in a language designed for it. Your example would look like this: export component Hello inherits Rectangle {\n in property <string> text: \"World\";\n width: 500px; height: 500px;\n background: #505050;\n border-width: 1px; border-color: blue;\n drop-shadow-blur: 15px; drop-shadow-color: #0008;\n VerticalLayout {\n alignment: center; spacing: 12px;\n Text { text: \"Hello, \\{root.text}!\"; font-size: 20px; color: white; horizontal-alignment: center; }\n HorizontalLayout {\n alignment: center; spacing: 8px;\n for c in [#f00, #0f0, #00f, #ff0, #000, #fff]: Rectangle { width: 32px; height: 32px; background: c; }\n }\n }\n }\n \n \nMore on why we chose a DSL vs code in Rust: https://slint.dev/blog/domain-specific-language-vs-imperativ... reply c-smile 23 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Rust isn't a great language for describing UIs because it is too explicit in places that doesn't need to be for coding a UI. That's is not about just Rust ... Any domain has its own optimal or sub-optimal language describing it. Criteria of optimality is a bit subjective of course. Like: INI, TOML, YAML, etc. are better (YMMV, again) for user facing configs than JSON or XML and so on. I think that HTML is better for defining hierarchical UI structures and CSS and CSS selectors are (arguably again) better to define UI styling. My Sciter[1] was initially using .FRM alike inputs (VisualBasic forms, good for IDE but bad for a human) but I quickly discovered that having styles, structure and code-behind-UI in single PL makes non-manageable mess. So Sciter uses HTML, CSS and JS we all familiar with. Not 100% match of course but good enough. [1] https://sciter.com reply ordu 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Surely there's a better way? I'd like to know it. I don't think that it can be substantially better. You have to compromise between the amount of customization you are allowed to do and the amount of options you have to pick values for. I can't vouch for GPUI, I don't know how good its compromise is, but it bears enough similarities to other modern UI-toolkits. I don't think there is a better way, but if you know one, I'd like to know about it. And, well, don't forget, it is a front-page example, that is not necessarily an example how one should compose their code, it demonstrates what you can do with GPUI. In real life code you'd probably move those children's construction into separate named functions, maybe you'd use some predefined styles to apply to individual elements, instead of picking fg/bg/text-colors, maybe sizes and some other attributes for each one explicitly, and maybe some other tricks. reply eviks 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n One improvement could be a better way to group and elide units, e.g. Instead of .border_1().border_color(rgb(0x0000ff)) You get .border(1 0x0000ff) (guess, this can only be in a macro) Or at least have \"color\" which understands that it's allied to border instead of having to repeat \"border\" > I don't think that it can be substantially better Don't give up too early, there is no way it can't be, we don't really have any good systems optimized for ergonomics to reach anything great reply c-smile 8 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > .border(1 0x0000ff) After 10 or so of those you'll start dreaming of border-left: 2px solid rgb(0 0 100%);\n \nright? reply tcallred 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n All a language needs to be good at expressing UIs is some sort of tail lambda syntax like Swift and Kotlin. It's the perfect way to express a tree structure (which is essentially all UI) while also being in a true programming language (as opposed to a markup language like XML). reply odo1242 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Well, keep in mind that you’re not expected to have the entire UI in one statement. You’re supposed to break this up into functions. That’s why stuff like Flutter and SwiftUI work. Also, this is basically what Tailwind code looks like. reply CupricTea 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This is basically what coding UI in Bevy was like before the `bsn!` macro reply kalleboo 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I'm never going to complain about SwiftUI again reply ijustlovemath 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n That's a result of the borrow checker meeting dev ergonomics. Method chaining/functional style ks extremely common. GPUI is also fairly low level, it lets you do a ton of things! reply desolate_muffin 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Someone needs close the loop and make an HTML/CSS to GPUI transpiler reply Jonovono 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You mean this? https://gpuix.dev reply satvikpendem 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n So, Blitz? It's by Dioxus Labs and converts HTML and CSS into native Rust rendering like GPUI. reply alexaholic 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n One person's mess is another person's Tailwind reply Swiffy0 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Not sure what to think of this. Sure, a Rust implementation will perform better and use less RAM, but it's quite obvious that the transition to Rust will be done almost entirely by AI. Don't get me wrong, the AI coded Rust version of Discord will most likely work just fine. The thing I'm wondering about is if it's a good idea to rewrite your whole codebase (which I assume is handwritten since Discord predates AI coding) with AI because now there is nobody that knows how it really works - I'm wondering what's the value of having a human-written codebase + the people who wrote it. Just feels like one shouldn't throw that into the trash like it's nothing? reply kombookcha 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I'm with you and I think a variety of companies are going to learn this in a highly expensive way over the next few years. Once your shared mental model of how the codebase works is gone it seems practically impossible to get it back. Like what would you even do if you change your mind in 3 years - sit everyone down to meticulously comb through all the AI code until they start to get how it works? Getting back to a similar level of understanding seems like it would take ages and effectively locks you into the new solution in a way that cannot be feasibly reversed. reply SoftTalker 13 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Also Reflections on Trusting Trust taken to a whole new level. reply stackbutterflow 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n How is this different from when you join a company and learn your corner of the codebase? In a sufficiently large enough codebase no one had the whole mental model in their head even before AI. What you did was find and ask that one person who knew enough about some part of the project to answer your questions. Now you can ask a model. reply theLiminator 3 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I believe institutional knowledge used to work on a ship of Theseus model. It's going to be completely different going forward. reply spunker540 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It seems like the value is having something performant. Their choices were 1) stick to a web framework their employees know well 2) a performant native app their employees don’t know as well. And they chose 2. If a model can tell you anything you’d hope to have a human tell you, what’s the difference? It’s a different point of failure— models can go offline, but employees can also quit anytime too. reply petra 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n When porting an app this way, don't functions/classes preserve the same functionality ? reply j_w 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Well not really? You are going from a webpage (Electron) to a native application via Rust. Say you port a React website to a Rust native application, is anything about that code actually similar? reply gjsman-1000 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Here's the critical assumption: Somebody has to know how it works, in order for it to be usable. That's not actually true. Nobody understands everything about how an airplane works, not even the lead engineers. Nobody understands everything about how a car works, or how medical billing works, or how taxes work, or how traffic flow works, or how a CPU works, or how almost anything works. You just need to understand the broad picture and be able to find the details when necessary. reply chneu 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Your post is absolute nonsense. Plenty of people know exactly how everything on a plane or car works, or a CPU. I've worked with tons of people who do. The fear of deskilling is very valid and is already messing things up. reply gjsman-1000 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Your post is absolute nonsense. Even the people you are referring to do not know how everything works. Does your airplane engineer know the exact tensile strength of the tire as it hits the landing strip? reply bccdee 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n They know the tires are rated for hitting a landing strip under XYZ conditions, they know how to test the tires to ensure they're still confirming to the expectations of that rating, and they're in contact with the trusted organization that's responsible for issuing that rating. Each expert has a comprehensive understanding of their field, and the fields comfortably overlap with no gaps. For any given perspective, there are several people with the complete picture of that perspective. Contrast this with a vibe-coded \"airplane\" where nobody actually understands the tires and you're basically operating on a set of assumptions. The tires are not rated and there's no trusted organization. Maybe it works, but you don't actually know. Very different; don't play language games. reply nozzlegear 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n You're putting forth a ridiculous argument. An airplane engineer doesn't need to know how to create the universe from scratch just to work their way up to maintaining airplanes, but they sure as hell understand how the major systems work, how those systems interact, and how to diagnose problems. They don't just tinker around blindly. reply subarctic 19 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n My understanding was that for every component there's someone somewhere who understands exactly how it works at least in terms of smaller components/abstractions and for each of those, recursively, someone knows how it works reply add-sub-mul-div 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n We're losing a respect and understanding of how basic competence works. reply wolvesechoes 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Nobody understands everything about how an airplane works, not even the lead engineers. Nobody understands everything about how a car works Of course people do. reply chasd00 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Doubtful. The engine engineer doesn’t know the chemical engineering of modern fuels nor the code in the engine controller. reply anthk 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n In my country in order to earn a true CS degree you need to learn both Chemistry and EE. Also check the Homebrew CPU running a custom port of Minix 2 with in-place ported C compilers, linkers and assemblers. reply rvz 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n This is textbook whataboutism. reply stasomatic 44 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n Who cares? I've never had performance issues with Slack, Discord and the like. It runs fast enough on my MBP. They could make it faster by removing the casino lights and prompts and upsells, and research other ways to monetize. Electron is not the issue with Discord, it's their constant annoying nagging. reply literallyroy 18 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n People playing games trying to squeeze out a few more frames. reply mintflow 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I noticed this news and because one of my codex banked reset is expiring soon I launch codex with astra+ultra on my tauri project because webview sometimes cause blank screen on user device to let it to rewrite the tauri projec to GPUI It did those stuffs for me - get rid of the CGO and MINGW toolchain for cross building windows on Linux - wrote a compatbility layer to communicate the original app backend written using rust - Achieve not ideal but acceptable UI/UX as the tauri project - The new build using 24MB memory which is quite good and I do not even bother to check the tauri version It amazing time to rewrite, though I still can't remember joy of old days that manual coding, but my lower back ask me to just embrace the Agentic coding... reply PreciousH 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n It's crazy how much RAM electron needs, at my old startup the main product was an electron app, i initially used to run into this issue where AI wouldn't clean up the dev builds it spunned up for e2e testing and each could be over 500MB, it just get's to a point that the cross platform advantage is no longer worth it, i think it was one of the main reasons cursor had to move off electron to rust based builds. reply pjmlp 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n It is a full browser, not surprising at all, only for those that never coded native applications. reply c-hendricks 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Oh if you don't like large build artifacts Tauri might not be something to get excited about. For reference, my src-tauri/target/ folder is 8.3gb. reply deaton 1 minute ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Its not Tauri, they're using GPUI reply Telaneo 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Holy Jesus of Nazareth. I love it. I just pray that Vencord will continue to work in some form, if only so I can use whatever font I want. I'm not a fan of many other changes they've made and I've thus had to reverse, but I'm willing to trade away the flexibility of CSS for an app that uses 5 times less RAM. Even better if it doesn't go away at all, but we'll see. reply RankingMember 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n It's great they're working on improving performance, but as they move towards an IPO I'd be looking hard at getting off their platform altogether. They're already shoehorning ads and all kinds of nonsense into their app- it's only going to get worse after the IPO. reply Telaneo 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Network effects keep me on it. I'm waiting for the day it collapses so I can spin up a Teamspeak (or whatever) server again, but it's just too darn convenient for everybody else, so I'm stuck with it unless I want to become even more of a hermit. reply Kuyawa 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n The app size also went from 10 GB to 10 MB, start time went from 10 secs to 10 ms Goodbye Electron, we barely knew you reply jttnr 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I wonder where the 10GB come from. Quick check on Flatpak shows 240 MB download and 568 MB installed size for the Linux install. https://flathub.org/en/apps/com.discordapp.Discord reply pjmlp 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I really hope with hardware prices going back to the last century, we finally get some best practices back. reply markbao 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Misinformation. Discord isn’t 10GB and nobody outside Discord has actually used the Rust port. reply hendrikmans 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Source? reply nticompass 37 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It's a joke. reply RunSet 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n In a perfect world it would be reduced to 0 MB. https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=30311982 reply hn773746483 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Discord even sends its DPO (data protection officer/privacy team) tickets to outsourced offshore copy & paste template support farms/AI chatbots. I've requested my data numerous times, half of the time the ticket was closed without a response, and the ones that DID get a response were the same copy & paste template telling me my data package is being generated. All data packages were missing 90% of my account data (bug? size issue with zip files?), no avenue to report this to Discord of course, because the only words their \"privacy team\" know is... :^) I was going to paste exactly what they sent me, but it turns out some of my data request tickets have been deleted by them. reply nonethewiser 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n What the fuck. That's amazing. 10GB->10MB can't simply be moving from Electron right? reply sgarland 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n This has to be hyperbole. I built (with Claude) a P2P LAN voice-only chat app in Rust recently, using native frameworks for each platform (Swift, GTK, Win32), and it weighs in at about 5-6 MB depending on the platform. I have spent an enormous amount of time looking for places to squeeze it further. It has ephemeral channels and password protection, but that’s about it: no text chat, no video, no friends, no blocklists, no concept of servers, etc. I can’t imagine everything else Discord has squeezes into another 5 MB. reply pjmlp 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Electron is a full Chrome instance, plus whatever else makes up the application. reply nonethewiser 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yes, and that only explains about 150MB. If it were simply the Chrome install that would imply Electron is 9GB+ by itself, which it most certainly is not. reply chii 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It's probably left over old instances and updates that werent deleted properly. These apps tend to download the update as an entire bundle, rather than any sort of binary diff and patch. Therefore, if you have an old install and have gone thru several (many) cycles of updates, the installation directory could be riddled with old versions and update files that make up the bulk of that 10 gig. reply hypfer 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It can and it does, because it drops a ton of code for scenarios that do not exist. (Not these numbers, of course, but if you check the comments of that account.. well. Yeah. Some kid) Electron rose to fame because one could just do everything with it and it was good enough. Being able to do everything also means that the thing can do everything everywhere all at once, which is many lines of code. ___ The magic of electron was that it was \"cheap\", however, with code being all AI-generated, that's even cheaper. So now everything will be some slop rust until the point when people realize that there is a cost to be paid after all. For electron, that has been RAM and performance. For the vibeslop.. well. We shall see. reply delta_p_delta_x 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Unironically, with LLMs, everyone should be looking to cut their system resource consumption (memory, disk, network, GPU, I/O traffic in general) and rewrite everything natively. Have a platform-independent business logic layer, and a platform-dependent UI/UX layer. Done. It's not even that hard, and has been done several times over; we quite literally have MVC/MVVM/etc that were invented two decades ago. Operating systems developers should be looking to profile everything . Benchmarking and profiling used to be horribly time-consuming; nowadays, with agents, this is almost trivial. Then valuable engineer time can be spent looking at actually solving and optimising the workloads, from the kernel, to drivers, to user-mode libraries, to vendored applications. About a decade ago programming was expensive, and we decided writing everything in JavaScript was OK, and wasted everyone's memory instead of using programmer time to write fast and efficient native applications. This is no longer the case. reply hypfer 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Unironically, with LLMs, everyone should be looking to cut their system resource consumption (memory, disk, network, GPU, I/O traffic in general) and rewrite everything natively. Depends. There is a lot of value in man-made software where the man knows exactly what happened where when why. If you just revibe in rust, you get something that works at this point in time, but this deep architectural knowledge is completely lost and you're at the mercy of Dario not locking down your account for wrongthink. That can still be fine for smaller tools where the cost of recreating them from scratch is alright, but revibing your core business thingy is likely a horrible idea, which I encourage people to do, as I'd love to have the business opportunity to take over their frustrated customerbase ca 5 years from now. reply delta_p_delta_x 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n