ns (time) to optimizing software are gone. If you want to do something impactful, but don't have any good ideas: Take an existing website or application and build an optimized version of it. If you aren't sure where to start, build an isolated workflow out of an existing app or site you like, have the LLM benchmark it, then ask it to make it use 10x less ram / run 10x faster. Ask about what tools it can use to measure, what benchmarks it can come up with to repeatedly test it, how it can automate workflows in a measurable way. Then turn those into tools or specs you can share along with your work. reply mahboi 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n With PCs, it was never about having a lot of RAM, it was about not having less RAM than others. So it's fine if everyone gets less RAM. reply HoldOnAMinute 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Sounds kind of socialist :-) \"No one gets TWO sticks of RAM until everyone has at least ONE stick of RAM!\" reply sharts 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n They’ll never give up bloated javascript frontends and call you poor for wanting anything else while sipping on their overpriced lattes. reply anthk 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I always cared with ZRAM under GNU/Linux (I own an n270 netbook with 1GB of RAM and a NUC with 8GB but a Pentium Silver CPU, nothing fancy). Thus, Waterfox with their new Rust based ad blocking engine was a beast, even more with WebGPU enabled and NoScript, just enabling JS for the essential, if any. On top of that, the tab suspending extension. As I program in Rust I need every bit of RAM, even if I disable most debugging options at ~/.config/cargo.toml and I set the build jobs to 1 with \"-j1 \". On the netbook, well.. CWM, UXterm, Dillo, CLI/TUI mail, usenet, IRC and so on clients, groff, nsxiv, mpv, MuPDF... it runs snappier than the NUC for what it does. Programming? Golang for networking stuff -security, not like Rust, but far better than C-, simple C99 for small software