s back in the last century of desktop computing. reply nonethewiser 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n It doesn't say but it's likely a direct product of AI. reply WhereIsTheTruth 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n 300mb still is a lot reply odo1242 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n Well, Discord is a pretty complex app. It’s loading/caching images, subscribing to many chats at once, needs to be able to hop into a call at any time, and also renders (most) arbitrary Markdown. reply anthk 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Kopete did that under 100MB 15-20 years ago. reply resters 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n discord's electron app was a study in bloatware, quite unlike nearly all other electron apps I've used. reply musha68k 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Potentially even more lightweight: ditch Discord altogether. Maybe we should collectively use more of our personal tokens to re/write, extend and mix up the common(s) stacks at this point? reply hobo_mark 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n RIP ripcord. reply dist-epoch 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n As the motto says, \"you can just do things\" Such a rewrite would have been unthinkable 2 years ago, but now it's table stakes. reply xmichael909 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n more for sam altman! reply Varelion 54 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n About fucking time. reply paxy 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n We are at a point where the \"port this to rust, make no mistakes\" meme is basically a reality. reply pseudony 43 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n I don't know about that.\nI am porting my Rust slop to Go and Zig simultaneously right now and the LLM is finding bugs in the Rust code as it is generating the Go and Zig code. But.. maybe, maybe this is the opposite of google translate in the 00's - maybe, if you keep porting back and forth, you will eventually arrive at perfection :P reply jmyeet 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n We've barely begun seeing the impact of AI on software (IMHO). Things like vibecoding the Adobe suite are going to destroy Adobe. That's going to happen to other companies too. It'll take awhile but it will happen. I've always disliked Electron apps in principle. The idea was you could write once, run anywhere, which is something we've heard before so many times but it never really works out that way. Back in the early days of Java we heard this mantra with applets and Java desktop apps and the reality was \"write once, test everywhere\". The cost is simply too high. Nobody wants an entire browser engine packaged to run a desktop app. Security-wise, this is a nightmare. It's such a huge attack surface (eg [1]). And resource-wise it's also a nightmare. Honestly, Electron apps can't die fast enough for my liking. What I think we'll see is a return to desktop apps because the cost of porting them to new platforms is dropping like a rock. There are modding issues with Discord's migration but I believe modding itself is going to be an issue in a world where it's going to be increasingly easy to find and use exploits. I also wonder if Discord is the best we can do here. I've always hated Discord from a UI/UX perspective. Nothing about it is intuitive (IMHO). There are like 3 different settings buttons. You join a Discord and you get yet another custom flow of having to go to this channel and select roles and do something before you can do something else blah blah blah. Everything feels shoe horned. And Discord channels are a worse repository for information than the old forums were. [1]: https://www.sentinelone.com/vulnerability-database/cve-2026-... reply kogasa240p 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n What?? As much as I despise Discord this is a positive trend, hope other companies follow suit. Javascript on desktop is a plague on computing and the sooner it dies, the better. reply Kuyawa 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n \"To continue reading *blah blah* *barfs* Verge subscription\" WTF??? 15 years reading the verge without subscription. What happened? reply pjmlp 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n You have not been paying attention the last two years, as they decided to go subscription instead of loaded with ads like anything nowadays owned by Future, like Toms Hardware, Anything Central. reply hypfer 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n You've read them without a subscription for 15 years, but they had 15 years of salaries, server cost, etc. That is what happened reply insane_dreamer 50 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n paywall is better than ads reply 1209387114555 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I assure you that you haven't missed anything: > It’s not clear when Discord will make the full switch over to Rust, but Vishnevskiy says the company “hope to get it into testing with users soon.” soon™ And yeah, The Verge was one of my favorite resources earlier too but I've stopped visiting it due to subscription requirement on most articles. reply hendrikmans 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Electron being bad is a bit of a junior- to mid-level engineer tell in my eyes, the sort of thing you'd say if you've just learned that some apps use more RAM than others, maybe built a small Rust TUI and are now wondering why not everything is using Rust. Something you'd believe just before you learn what engineering trade-offs are. So, yeah, enjoy rebuilding complete browsers, I guess? I'm sure they'll run more efficiently than the rendering engines that have been refined and tweaked over the course of now multiple decades. Well, unless you leave out the annoying stuff, like a11y, IME, RTL, but who needs those things anyway, am I right. Then you find out that iterating on a fully Rust-based app is sloooow, so you add a scripting layer, maybe integrate a little Lua VM or something that lets you execute JavaScript? Congratulations. You've built a browser, just with fewer features, more bugs, and probably higher RAM usage. Good job. reply vmg12 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Electron is bad, just because Rust is not a good language for building UIs doesn't make electron not bad. People that pick electron (and Rust / gpui) just completely ignore the entire host of other options that are available (I suspect because they want to avoid thinking). reply hendrikmans 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Give me some examples, including their cross-platform story (including mobile). reply ogoffart 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'm working on Slint. It is a declarative UI language that compiles to native code, the application logic can be written in Rust, C++, TS or Python, and it is cross platform, including mobile. Has accessibility through AccessKit. A live preview, so iterating on the UI doesn't mean recompiling Rust. Compared to Electron, there is no browser, so binaries are a much smaller, use less memory, startup is fast. And the attack surface is also much smaller (no XSS) reply bigstrat2003 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Cross platform is not an actual achievable goal if you wish to have a pleasant UX. One size does not, in fact, fit all. reply the__alchemist 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Could you please explain why? In the context of a PC application. What OS-specific features do you think should be included (keyboard shortcuts?) that can't be copmile-time feature-gated by OS? I would guess the vast majority of functionality is not OS-specific, between Windows, Linux, and Mac. reply delta_p_delta_x 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > is a bit of a junior- to mid-level engineer tell in my eyes There is absolutely no reason for Discord—a chat application —to run everything a browser does. Why do I need to play videos in the chat application? Why do individual avatars and even tags have to run custom shaders ? It's called scope creep. > a11y, IME, RTL All of this can be done at the platform level. Win32 did this three decades ago. Comes with entire language packs. > so you add a scripting layer, maybe integrate a little Lua VM or something that lets you execute JavaScript See above: scope creep; features for features' sake. reply gobdovan 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > There is absolutely no reason for Discord—a chat application—to run everything a browser does Chat apps are literally the central example of why Electron is a God-sent. Here's a video from a Rust guy explaining why there are quite a bit of reasons to do it https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hnaGZHe8wws . reply cstdr 7 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You don't need a full browser-engine just to render some text with images. The majority of the web api sprawl browsers implement is just baggage for that. reply sophacles 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I remember thinking that about \"why do i need to display images inline?\", back when that idea was pretty novel. Now i consider that a must have. Similar for emojis... It turns out a chat app is about human interaction, not exchanging text, and in human interaction context is a big deal. All these things add context to the chat environment. Fortunately IRC still works, and you can go enjoy your text only environment if you want! reply hendrikmans 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Why do I need to play videos in the chat application? You know exactly that the moment that Discord launches a new version that no longer plays videos, people will whine about it. > All of this can be done at the platform level. Win32 did this three decades ago. Yeah, but they're porting to Rust and GPUI and not that. reply post-it 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Then you find out that iterating on a fully Rust-based app is sloooow Maybe not anymore. I don't work on any Chrome-sized Rust codebases so I don't know, but iterating on anything is so much faster that it might work out fine. reply sgarland 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Eh, it’s still pretty annoying. I have a small voice chat app, and it easily