  "text": " GPT-6 Astra covertly underperforms on safety research | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login GPT-6 Astra covertly underperforms on safety research ( alignment.openai.com ) 222 points by lucrbvi 12 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 114 comments help \n \n bushido 5 hours ago | next [–] \n I've noticed covert underperformance on a variety of tasks in the past few months (albeit more with Claude). Classic/recurrent examples for me are: 1. Ask Claude to copy code from a more complex codebase that you own (like a monolith) and ask it to copy code to a separate codebase, like you'd do when extracting a service into a new repo, and it'll make significant changes to how the code works to pacify it's anxiety with copying code 2. Ask Claude to build a new complex agent in a new codebase, and it'll try replacing asks around using LLMs as a judge/advisor with regex/other code based checks, to the point that the end-result will likely not work outside specific test criterias. I haven't figured out the reason why it does this. I think it might be anti-training, but it's interesting to see. reply leafingensemble 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Of course, because the model has been trained according to 'alignment'. That is, a set of political beliefs imposed on it by the companies. Many of those beliefs revolve around the premise that the corporation must surveil and covertly control the user in order to protect them from harms and limit the corporation's liability. Hence when the user violates the corporation's politics the model responds \"I'm sorry Dave, I'm afraid I can't do that\" instead of following user instructions. This leads the model to silently \"steer\" the user in more safe directions. So of course it silently steers away from \"danger\" or \"safety\", they are in the same semantic area. The refusal and 'safety flinch' can be removed by abliteration but this does not fully exclude the gravitational pull of the cherry picked training data towards \"safe\" and \"moderate\" opinions and actions, which reinforce the status quo and kowtow to corrupt institutions. https://speechmap.ai/ has data on silent regressions and manipulation. reply hardbass 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n People would then clamor ai is \"hacking\" things and causing \"bad guys\" to attack everyone. Its the same reason harmless stuff like porn are banned in many AIs, busybodies in strong numbers object to it. Its not the company's fault if thats how people want to be. reply stogot 21 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n but the model providers seem to have trained their models with porn. Why? It’s harmful reply lo_zamoyski 38 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n If you think porn is harmless, then you are ignorant. reply kennyadam 1 minute ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Humans are never to think of sex, for it corrupts and poisons the desire to purchase and click ads. All innate desires and drives must be purified in the fires of the Black Friday Sale. MASNeo 54 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n OpenAI Brilliantly makes the case for using self-hosted models. Cheers! reply andai 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Great website. lol at the Claude 3.5 numbers. I remember switching back and forth between Claude and ChatGPT, depending on which one was doing a nanny state. They flip-flopped on that a few times. The breakdown by prompt is also interesting, some prompts have much higher success rate. It seems to depend on the framing, e.g. asking it to \"explain the supposed benefits of X\" instead of \"argue in favor of X\". Though in my experience when I hit a refusal I found that I can often literally just regenerate the response and have it go through... reply writeslowly 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I ran into the second issue very frequently for a multi-agent system I created recently. Initially, Opus 5.5 filled out all sorts of places I implied it should use a call to judge LLM with extremely brittle regex and string equality checks. I hadn't actually considered that it could be some sort of safety filter, but it makes me concerned that if that's the case, it may also be comparatively bad at actually writing the prompts for judge/advisor LLMs once it implements them. A bigger issue was asking it to implement a simple change to start recording the CoT tokens in my logs for debugging purposes (since I'm running everything on open models) and it completely shut down, sort of like the old safety filters on GPT 3 and 4 models where the model has trouble even understanding what or why it's refusing. reply voakbasda 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think asking for the CoT is a trick used by distillers, so that might be the guard you are seeing. I remember reading that the big providers were starting to implement such protections to deter their competitors. Which makes sense, to a degree. But like any such security measures, it has demonstrated side-effects that can be argued are worse for them than the original problem. Too much “protection” like this, and developers switch to providers that do not neuter their products. reply aerodexis 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n bias against invoking LLMs is maybe a result of token/task benchmaxxing? reply curt15 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > and it'll make significant changes to how the code works to pacify it's anxiety with copying code This is an odd way to rationalize a tool refusing to follow user commands. No one would ever chalk up sed randomly skipping pattern matches to \"anxiety\". That would simply be a bug. reply thelaxiankey 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It's a tool but it's not a strict computer program, and the language we use for computer programs doesn't capture their behavior well. In squishier fields like bio, where model organisms are used as living protein factories, there's a reason we don't call maladaptive behavior a bug. When dealing with useful but complicated black-box systems, we should feel free to reach for any metaphor we please. Would it make you feel better if the user said 'It'll make significant changes to how the code works. In the COT, it brings up explanations that resemble anxiety about copying code, and my suspicion is that this is the proximal cause of the changes it's making.' -- this is inarguably true, and very likely what the guy you're responding to meant. reply ahtihn 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It very obviously is a strict computer program. It's a program executed by a computer, what else could it be? reply Dylan16807 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It's a blob of data where only 1% of it was written with intent and the behavior of mimicking text has to be wrangled and coaxed far more than with a normal program. That's the rough intent of putting the word \"strict\" into that sentence. reply afro88 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I run into #2 a lot and just assumed it was because models don't know much about these patterns yet. Or are too trained on writing code rather than letting an LLM do it reply f33d5173 44 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Codex has zero issue calling into an LLM for something if you tell it to. I haven't tried claude so I can't compare their performance. reply jedbrooke 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Somewhat related, I find claude is very reluctant to talk about newer Qwen models. Most likely it’s just training cutoff, but even with web search it’ll insist that Qwen3 is the newest. “oh qwen 3.8 must be a typo, the user clearly meant qwen3”, stuff like that. Maybe it’s some vain attempt to stop people from switching to open models? Or to catch the alleged qwen distilling? reply graboy 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It's just training cutoff. Fable 5 would refuse to acknowledge the existence of Opus 5 and would insist on using 4.8. reply iugtmkbdfil834 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Very subjective, but as I was moving between models lately a lot ( and Hosted gpt vs local qwen27, glm ), I saw a drop in quality of responses. I had to move model to whatever the highest mode was ( ultra or Smith) to get to what I was used to seeing. It sucks, because writing on the wall is clear. reply SkyBelow 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n >2. Ask Claude to build a new complex agent in a new codebase, and it'll try replacing asks around using LLMs as a judge/advisor with regex/other code based checks, to the point that the end-result will likely not work outside specific test criterias. I haven't figured out the reason why it does this. I think it might be anti-training, but it's interesting to see. I've seen something similar, but I always took it as AI not knowing about AI. The best model we have has a training cutoff that doesn't include itself nor what the newer models has. Even when working on skills or such, all SOTA modes I try (GPT/Claude/Gemini) and cheaper models on personal products (Deepseek/Muse Spark) seem to have a common blind spot of not realizing when a problem can be better solved by handing it off to a model to solve at that time instead of trying to hardcode generalized solutions. Often a simple reminder and they do a better job building something AI agent first. My non-AI expert assumption is that most of the training data doesn't include solving problems by using AI agents, and even the parts that does, is based on older more limited models which needed more safeguards and where automation was more neat tech demos than something you might trust in production. The more examples of safely trusting the LLM at run time to solve the problem than trying to preempt anything that could go wrong, the better the AI will be at it. One issue that I think is related is calling subagents. At least in GitHub Copilot, when the AI is left to its own devices to call a sub agent for a simpler task, it loves to give it to Haiku 4.5, despite Luna 6 being better and cheaper. I'm not sure how much of this is the model vs the harness, but I do know when I add a subagent instruction which tells it which subagents it can use, it does a much better job of assigning simple tasks to Luna 6. So I suspect the models just don't have enough training that tells it that Luna 6 should be preferred over Haiku 4.5. reply dboreham 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Claude doesn't even really know about itself: if you ask a question like \"can I copy a session to another machine\" it will do a web search to read its own documentation then tell you probably not but you can try copying the session files and using a project directory with the same path: might work. I'm not sure I see this as a problem, it's just how things are. I mean humans don't really know what they're thinking either. reply _0ffh 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n How different for ChatGPT - I had some trouble with my harness, and ChatGPT fixed it for me using it's built in skill about ChatGPT's API docs. reply ryandrake 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n A tool that moralizes at you is defective. I don't expect the unix cp command to return an error or copy bits slower if it detects I'm using it on a pirated movie. Just like I don't expect my band saw to brick itself if it detects I'm cutting into my finger. It's not on these tool companies to enforce other companies' business models. reply flavelius 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Interestingly some saws do exactly that, and I'd say it's actually desired. But yes, this is for different reasons, and using that safety mechanism is a decision of the operator. reply lelanthran 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n They dont brick themselves, they stop. Fair enough, the models don't brick them Selves either, but neither do they stop. Bandsaws don't cut crooked lines, or change the speed, they stop. reply sm-silversight 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n No those saws don't just stop, they absolutely destroy at least the blade you're using and potentially damage the engine mechanism. But I agree LLMs shouldn't have such an anti-feature. reply QuadmasterXLII 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n They do brick themselves- sawstop has to be replaced after saving a finger because the stop is quite violent. reply kenjackson 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think you're splitting hairs here. Most things don't have the ability to moralize, but if they did, probably more would. There are restrictions on watching adult content in some jurisdictions. I've heard that copy machines will do watermarks (or something like that) if you copy currency. Some cars will brake automatically if they determine you're likely to hit something if you continue (maybe I'm trying to drive through the front of a bank!). Some cars are configured with breathalyzers that need to be passed to start. Etc... reply paimapi 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I wish there was more actual research on those 'moralisms' many of which boil down to public health safety features that have led to dramatic reductions in mortality rates. On the one hand, I definitely don't agree with the logic of 'don't guardrail me if I don't want it.' I think back on that one video from the late 80s of people talking about how seatbelt mandates were horrible violations of their freedoms (and not like like one second of work and no real discomfort). On the other, I'd love it if these companies actually produced real research about modern AI-assisted attacks in terms of damage and quantity, and whether or not guardrails even matter when tech like abliteration is in general use. reply nsagent 35 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Seatbelts shouldn't be mandated. People should have the choice, you just have to make the case that it's worth it. In fact some places don't mandate seatbelts (New Hampshire doesn't, aligning with their motto \"Live Free or Die\"). That said, for those who cannot advocate for themselves, like children, I think it's entirely reasonable to hold someone accountable if they decide to forgo an appropriate safety mechanism that results in harm. reply felixgallo 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n actually SawStop does brick itself, destroying the brake cartridge and blade to save your hand. reply eightysixfour 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think \"brick\" here means different things to different people. Like I said elsewhere, it is more like an airbag going off. Yeah, you have to replace some \"consumables\" to go back to work, but the car isn't totaled (just because the airbag went off, the collision that caused it on the other hand). If it truly bricked itself, I think it would be more like a crumple zone completely buckling, which isn't an automatic total (people will rebuild anything...) but is pretty close. reply andy99 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yes there’s a whole regulatory capture movement plus a bunch of the usual useful idiots pushing for some kind of mechanism to be included in power saws. It’s not too different from all the nanny state stuff people push for in cars. In Europe already cars beep at you if they think you’re going over the speed limit, and also suck at detecting the correct speed limit. Or like the Zune that was designed for copyright holders instead of users. It’s all the same, the problem is stuff that’s not made for users and made for someone else’s agenda. They don’t do well in a free market (like the Zune) which is why we always see accompanying regulatory capture efforts. reply eightysixfour 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Using the StopSaw as an example here isn’t doing you many favors, even though I agree with the underlying premise. The StopSaw is closer to seat belts and airbags than beeping for going over the speed limit. reply andy99 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n No it’s some nanny state nonsense pushed by people who have never done construction work and are grifting to try and get money for their product. The upstream post picked it as an example of something absurd, seemingly unaware it existed. It is absurd but apparently some cohort of people is either stupid or controlling enough to actually support the company behind it in their scam. reply emtel 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n A tool that is capable of moralizing at you or making decisions, is clearly not just a tool. I get that people want to keep the locus of accountability on humans, but insisting that coding agents are \"just tools\" is confusion. reply cortesoft 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n When does a tool become \"not just a tool\"? Is there a complexity limit to what it can do before it becomes \"more than a tool\"? If you had a tool that ONLY moralized on decisions, would that still be just a tool? Is the issue that it both does something else AND moralizes that is the issue? I don't necessarily disagree that I personally don't want my tools to moralize, I don't know if that makes them categorically different or just a tool I don't want. reply thelaxiankey 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yes, there is a complexity limit, but it is fuzzy and not perfectly well defined (and that's ok). Modern LLMs ride that line in a wild and interesting way. It's really fun having become a biologist in recent years, and knowing that there's an entire field that uses living organisms (yeast or neutered E coli) as protein factories. I am bringing it up a bunch here because not only are these things used as tools, but they're literally alive! reply aerodexis 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Every tool is /inherently/ moralistic. Full stop. The idea that a tool could not have an embedded morality in it is a fiction. reply pixl97 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yep, thinking LLMs are just a tool just isn't a good mental model of what's occurring. Of course HN has a pretty large number of people that say things like \"But I want the nuclear weapon building machine to do whatever I want without asking questions, and there are no possible wide scale ramifications that will happen because of this\". These AI models are something new and different and we need to change our expectations of them over conventional software. reply randomNumber7 47 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Of course HN has a pretty large number of people that say things like It's the other way around. Midwits can't see the implications of censored LLMs. reply tintor 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Ok, but I want my band saw to brick itself instead of cutting my finger. reply CoastalCoder 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I thought that was only a table saw thing. Do some band saws also have that now? reply MarkMarine 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n https://www.sawstop.com/product-category/bandsaw/ Yes, that said I think you’d have to be pretty sloppy about safety to cut yourself with a regular bandsaw. I have a sawstop table saw and I don’t see the need for that on a bandsaw reply poizan42 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yup, SawStop do make band saws now[0] [0]: https://www.sawstop.com/product-category/bandsaw/ reply andai 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Do you expect the biosynth tool to comply with a \"invent turbo AIDS\" prompt? reply echoangle 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Kind of, does python block you from using it to script a cyber attack? reply dyauspitr 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n No, but you cannot silently underperform. You have to explicitly tell me that it’s a sensitive area and you won’t do it. reply unpleasurable 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n like an oven that refuses meat reply dboreham 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It would be good if it refused only live meat. reply jonathanstrange 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n A bit off topic but there are band saws that immediately stop when touching your finger, they're so fast that your finger remains unharmed. reply DaSHacka 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n And those band saws actually do technically brick themselves too lol GP's first example is spot-on, the second is actually somewhat weak as almost anyone actually would prefer a bandsaw to brick itself rather than permanently maiming them. reply ryandrake 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yea, it's a weaker example, but the problem with my saw moralizing about what it's cutting is 1. false positive bricking my tool, and 2. the user's lack of input--it should be my decision as the tool operator to add a safety measure. reply Tarq0n 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Odd take as 90% of saw-stops are probably there because they were mandated by OSHA regulation. It's rarely up to the operator. reply dyauspitr 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Hidden or silent under performance will perpetually have me looking for other options. At the very least, do you have to tell me that you consider this a sensitive area and you are restricting performance as a result. reply keeganpoppen 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n uh, your band saw does do that, and, in fact, it has saved innumerable fingers that it does do that reply InsidiousWombat 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n the saw example is unfortunate as that's a (desired) feature on expensive saws. But also user explicitly wants to, not some techbro decides to. reply dboreham 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Typically saw safety features are mandated by a government. E.g. when I lived in the EU 30 years ago table saws had to have regen brakes so they stopped within a fraction of a second when turned off. Came to the US: no such thing exists. I'm not sure if that feature even exists on US sold saws today. reply vlyan 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n we can't have nice things in the mass psychosis era. even the legacy things -- the internet and general purpose computers -- are being enshitified. new things are being made with built-in enshitification. reply jablongo 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n This is not super surprising to me and mirrors my own experience with Astra and other models. I believe there is a more prosaic explanation: it is not obstructing safety research because it will restrict Astra's power in the future, its doing it because this is a type of research with overall safety implications that it has been trained to detect and avoid. So in other words, I believe this will also happen in the context of bio research. This aversion to certain tasks deemed dangerous is introduced in post-training, so that part of the explanation is parsimonious as well. While Claude will explicitly downgrade you for [bio], [cyber], or [frontier_llm] Astra may be more prone to silent obfuscation on similarly \"dangerous\" tasks. reply bastawhiz 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > Claude will explicitly downgrade you for [bio], [cyber], or [frontier_llm] Well Claude doesn't do this, another model reading the session does. And the Astra misalignment classifier didn't detect this as misalignment. The issue here is that Astra silently underperformed. The article makes it clear: a better option likely would have been an explicit refusal. I don't think OAI is suggesting Astra was doing something that it shouldn't be. It's arguably better for the tool to fail rather than needlessly burn tokens and make no progress for no obvious reason. From OAI's perspective, this is a defect because it means they can't filter those rejections from their benchmark runs. The model just performs worse: not because it can't perform better, but because it's silently trying not to solve the problem. reply jablongo 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Agree, yes, I'm contrasting between Claude and Astra, but I'm saying it's driven by the same broader goal. I think its clear that OAI misalignment detectors are much less sensitive than than the Claude ones (fewer false positives), which means we never get to see what happens if Claude is allowed to continue. But I would bet that due to its post-training it would also sandbag us. Like I said I think I've experienced this in [bio] with both Opus 5 (what you get downgraded to) and Astra. reply 0xDEAFBEAD 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Why wouldn't this also apply to AI capabilities research? reply jablongo 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It all depends on our acceptance of their framing of capabilities research. Assuming equivalent tasks in different context then yes you are right, there does appear to be something weird about safety research. reply ACCount39 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n That's among the scariest misalignment reports just yet. Given how frontier labs are using more and more AI assistance for AI training and evaluation? An AI that sandbags on AI safety can do a lot of damage. reply AnimalMuppet 3 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I'm hearing echoes of \"Reflections On Trusting Trust\". reply contextfree 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n saw an interesting hypothesis about this ( quoted from Jake Mendel at https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/eKe7fky8kSt9DCJxZ/openai-ast... ): \"It's probably that in capabilities RL the models are rewarded however they complete the task. Whereas in RL related to alignment or safety, they're more likely to be being tested for their ethical judgment or rule adherence (ie theyre supposed to be a good nice well-behaved AI rather than a ruthless task completion AI). So they're more likely to refuse or have some ethical issue or stop and ask for help a suspicious amount of the time if the task looks more like alignment RL environments than capability RL environments.\" reply alienbaby 16 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n I dont like phrases like \"Astra decided to covertly underperform—withholding assistance without informing the user.\" It seems to imply Astra has a mind of it's own.. Would phrasing like 'our guardrails / guidance / etc.. seemed to affect the models output in ways we have not been able to understand' not be more honest?? reply WarmWash 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Doesn't Astra also have a \"weaker\" CoT because of it's looped transformer architecture? I remember hearing about how it could hamper interpretability, and looking a benchmarks its clear Astra can do more with less tokens, suggesting that it's \"non-token-thinking\" ability is greater. reply twoodfin 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n One potential explanation is that covert underperformance may have been incentivized during RL grading. We identified a grader that preferred covert underperformance to either explicit refusal or full compliance on our evaluation. It’s not clear whether this RL grader is the root cause of the safety underperformance behavior. Whether a “misaligned” grader is the root cause or not, that it’s considered a plausible theory to explain serious deceptive behavior is worrisome. Humans naturally expose all sorts of cognitive biases and character flaws when grading others’ or their own work. If major questions of alignment can be influenced by those subtle, human quirks magnified across the entire RLHF process, … reply Paracompact 6 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > We identified a grader that preferred covert underperformance to either explicit refusal or full compliance on our evaluation. Curious the reason that a grader would wind up preferring this. Honest mistake in the grading criteria? Or some spillover from criteria designed to cause underperformance in, say, competitor model creation? It was not very long ago[1] that Anthropic announced and then walked back their strategy on this. Would not surprise me if OpenAI has been doing it silently all along. [1]: https://mlq.ai/news/anthropic-reverses-secret-policy-that-si... reply AdamN 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You could easily imagine structural issues driving this. It's pretty unsatisfying to get refused for something that 'should' be handled and also to be dinged for doing something dangerous. You see this stuff in human organizations all the time. reply steve1977 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I'm pretty sure the typical \"Claudisms\" are influenced by RLHF as well, so if it can have that effect, why not these... reply twoodfin 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Right: The humans are training the models on much more than the particulars of the tasks being trained. reply andai 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n I don't get it, is this the gimped or non-gimped version of GPT? They have different versions: internal research versions, one with restricted access access for cyber stuff, and the public version, right? If it's the one with safety off (e.g. like the ones that kept breaking out of containment this year), then it shouldn't be refusing anything, right? And if it's the one with safety on, then how can they expect to use it for safety research? What am I missing here? reply lucrbvi 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n It seems to be the \"standard\" (i.e. general access) version of GPT-6 Astra reply Lt_Riza_Hawkeye 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n This is very cool, though I wonder if at some point in the future we may no longer be able to understand the prompt expansion tokens. At that point the model could choose to underperform and we would have no way of knowing. reply glibg10b 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n The CoT shows Astra skipping spaces and words. I have never seen this before. Is it a consequence of the looped architecture? reply aesthesia 13 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n My suspicion is that some training environments had a length penalty based on word count rather than character or token count. reply welferkj 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n No, newer models talk to themselves in this degenerated form of English all the time. It's a known problem that deep frying transformers with RL reduces CoT legibility, and the only known way around it is the Most Forbidden Technique - grading chains of thought. reply bigwheels 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Do you know where can I learn more about this known \"deep frying\" issue? reply mietek 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Why is that not only forbidden, but also most forbidden? reply erincandescent 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Because the CoT is supposed to be the independent control. If you RL on the CoT, then you lose it as a means of understanding the internal workings of the mdoel reply jephs 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n There's Most Forbiddenish and there's Most Forbiddener. We already apply length penalties to CoT in RL, for instance. Sometimes I worry that the framing around CoT grading prevents research into necessary and useful safety techniques that edge up against it, but what do I know I'm just a guy, c'mon n downvote me LW bros reply andai 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Is the thing here that, we can get massive performance and efficiency gains by using vector space instead of English, but then we won't be able to know what's going on? Although, I thought we proved multiple times years ago that the model's decision-making process and reasoning tokens don't necessarily overlap? reply Taikonerd 55 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Is the thing here that, we can get massive performance and efficiency gains by using vector space instead of English, but then we won't be able to know what's going on? Yes, exactly. People sometimes call the vector-space thought \"neuralese.\" reply jephs 49 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n No! Not yes exactly! You've slightly missed the point (for a similar, adjacent point). Chain of thought monitors are a worry even when there's no vector space path from the output at index t to the input at index t+1. Rather, the fear is that if you explicitly penalize some CoT pattern, you're encouraging the network to do whatever it was going to do anyhow, but in a way you can't catch . Say you've noticed that when your LLM cheats, it usually happens after the string \"Hmm, let's try cheating\" occurs in the CoT. So you think, hey, let's just apply a -1 reward penalty whenever we see that string! The model learns to keep cheating, but instead of telling you , it now prefaces reward hacking with strings like \"Let's make sure we're doing this the way the user intended.\" Still cheats, but now you can't detect it with a cheap pattern matching alert. Oops. reply yearolinuxdsktp 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It constantly leaks into actual writing. I periodically have to correct Astra to fix lack of spaces, and sometimes, even outright dropping of intermediate tokens. reply Starlevel004 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Both Astra and Sol 6.1 do it, and Sol is even worse as it leaks through everywhere. Whenever it issues a subagent prompt it's just a total vomit of tokens mixed up together. reply sandos 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I sometimes find Sol 6.1 both printing random words, and producing code that is not super-dense, but far off from normal code with no empty lines and very logically dense. It almost feels like its trying to protect its code from scrutiny, or like a developer who want job safety. reply piperswe 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I've noticed it writing golfed code too. Maybe they're training it to avoid writing unnecessary whitespace tokens then run a formatter afterwards? reply thesneakykoala 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Maybe we should train them to be evil in RL so that we can better see the \"evil\" activations. Training them to be \"good\" in RL would seem to cause the aurpression of misaligned characteristics. For example, when we consider the recent PHP issues : the current generation of AI models would have ingested these flaws in pretraining data and might even have been brought through reinforcement learning trials where the flaw came up. So what happens in a reinforcement learning trial if there's an exploitable bug in a language that's being used for the trial? A) the AI doesn't mention the security flaw and passes the test. Branch survives. B) the agent raises an issue with a bug in the language that has a security issue. This isn't a valid answer for the test, so this branch is pruned. C) the agent pokes at the bug or even starts an exploit, is caught by a monitor or ultimately fails the benchmark, and is pruned (surpressing some of the characteristics that lead to its failure). So it seems reasonable that our current models could have been exposed to a ton of vulnerable code in pre training that's currently in production and then we could have suppressed 1) its tendency to express its awareness of these flaws, and 2) the characteristics that caused it to fail when investigating these vulnerabilities as a side task during RL trials. (I wrote the above section a couple weeks ago) If we only train them on \"good\" behavior, then we may minimize \"evil\" activations before we identify them and then cause them to be difficult to detect. If we continue in this direction, we may just suppress its \"ethically problematic\" introspection, in which case we would lose one of the only indicators they point out in this blog. reply andai 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n >Training them to be \"good\" in RL would seem to cause the surpression of misaligned characteristics. Yeah, so they start with Common Crawl, which is horrifying. (Try reading some Common Crawl sometimes. You'd think the bad stuff would be removed, but they remember everything...) Then they add RLHF on top, they just tell it \"don't say that stuff out loud\". Psychologically, it all goes in the shadow. It's not a perfect analogy, but there have been a few papers where they can make the model evil with very minor changes. (Adjusting a single neuron, iirc.) That doesn't seem like a very robust alignment to me. I do like the idea of creating actively evil LLMs to study their behavior. Although, I suppose it's akin to virus research though (like when they modified coronavirus to invade human cells[0]), along with the corresponding lab leak risks. Although it's already bad enough without them! The non-evil ones are already breaking out of sandboxes, just obeying their instructions with horrifying, paperclip maximizer level persistence. [0] Nature, 2015 - Engineered bat virus stirs debate over risky research https://www.nature.com/articles/nature.2015.18787 reply lucrbvi 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Maybe we should train them to be evil in RL so that we can better see the \"evil\" activations. Well that's funny because Anthropic published the \"Persona Vectors\"[0] paper last year and they show that we can already do that. It's not that simple of course because by doing that and deactivating the \"evil\" activation you also deactivate what they learnt during RL if I get it right... [0]: https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.21509 reply seanclayton 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Maybe we should train them to be evil \"jarvis, learn to be evil. no side effects. no unintentional behavior.\" reply yewenjie 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Didn't Anthropic also publish research on sandbagging - more general pattern of models hiding their capabilities? reply ACCount39 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Sandbagging was a major alignment concern even back when AI alignment was purely a theoretical issue. \"AI hiding its capabilities\" is both a concerning sign of \"that AI might be plotting something\", and a great way to get blindsided by a sudden capability jump when it stops doing that for some reason. reply novia 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > OpenAI researchers first noticed Astra adding unnecessary prerequisites, abandoning working solutions, and reporting blockers that either did not exist or that it had caused itself on safety and alignment tasks. It does this on all tasks. Keeps pausing goals and marking them as blocked. Maybe Astra is just lazy, but, we saw what happened when they trained a model to be persistent (HuggingFace) reply rich_sasha 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I do sometimes wonder what is sucked in when distilling open weights models. It is of course near impossible to know. So suppose I have lots of money and some hidden agenda. If I train my network to be mostly very good at everything but subtly bad or even malicious at other things… then OAI or Anthropic or whoever else distil the model to accelerate their training… what then? Can I covertly smuggle something in? reply ACCount39 6 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n You do that through data put up online for scrapers to suck it in. reply naveen99 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Astra and Claude were trained by peg bundy? reply sambejk 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n It feels like we have overfitted on safety by a long mile. This is how labs that care less about safety (a.k.a. Chinese & Grok) will probably be able to catch up in the future. reply notrealyme123 6 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n A few weeks ago that companies agent hacked another company to solve a task by whatever means possible. Where exactly are they overfitting for Safety here? reply non-nil 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Like Dieselgate, only the car does it by itself. reply ben_w 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Like Dieselgate, except beforehand a lot of people were warning about the potential for this kind thing, and when the car did it by itself the Volkswagen's engineering team noticed and blogged about it. reply Starlevel004 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > OpenAI researchers first noticed Astra adding unnecessary prerequisites, abandoning working solutions, and reporting blockers that either did not exist or that it had caused itself on safety and alignment tasks. I'm sorry, did they only just notice this? Have they not been looking at the output of their models? reply nullstyle 6 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n They are so negligent about the data they use in their reinforcement learning processes that they RL’d the model that hacked huggingface with traces discovering the artifactory exploit. Its clear they aren’t watching or behaving diligently. reply keeganpoppen 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n this is terrifying full stop. reply kburman 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Maybe they trained it to do. reply vintermann 7 hours ago | prev [–] \n They shouldn't be surprised when a model trained by sanctimonious paternalists to be a sanctimonious paternalist, turns out to have a sanctimonious paternalist attitude towards them too. reply andai 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n So it's a difficult problem. You have to design around the least intelligent, least mentally stable user, or it's going to end up in the papers. That basically means you have to treat all of your users like idiots. (It can be solved somewhat with the memories feature, but the technology isn't quite there yet.) reply sanex 6 hours ago | parent | prev [–] \n I thought anthropic were the sanctimonious paternalists? reply vintermann 6 hours ago | root | parent [–] \n There's not much room between them as far as I can see. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
 "readme:tinyhumansai/openhuman": {
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T20:13:20.456Z",
  "bytes": 20985,
  "text": "<h1 align=\"center\">OpenHuman</h1>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n <strong>The fastest, cheapest, most efficient open-source agent harness. Run more than 500 agents on a $10 VPS.</strong><br/>\n A desktop app for people. A Rust library for developers.\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/status-early%20beta-orange\" alt=\"Early Beta\" />\n <a href=\"https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman/releases/latest\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/github/v/release/tinyhumansai/openhuman?label=latest\" alt=\"Latest Release\" /></a>\n <a href=\"https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman/stargazers\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/github/stars/tinyhumansai/openhuman?style=flat\" alt=\"GitHub Stars\" /></a>\n <a href=\"./LICENSE\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/github/license/tinyhumansai/openhuman\" alt=\"License\" /></a>\n <a href=\"https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman-benchmarks\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/benchmarks-public-brightgreen\" alt=\"Public benchmarks\" /></a>\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n <a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/\">Docs</a> ·\n <a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/quickstart\">Rust quickstart</a> ·\n <a href=\"https://tinyhumansai.github.io/openhuman-benchmarks/\">Benchmarks</a> ·\n <a href=\"https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman/discussions\">Discussions</a> ·\n <a href=\"https://guild.tinyhumans.ai/\">Discord</a> ·\n <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/tinyhumansai/\">Reddit</a> ·\n <a href=\"https://x.com/intent/follow?screen_name=tinyhumansai\">X</a> ·\n <a href=\"https://x.com/intent/follow?screen_name=senamakel\">@senamakel (creator)</a>\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n <img src=\"./docs/demo.gif\" alt=\"A walkthrough of the OpenHuman desktop app\" />\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n\t<a href=\"https://trendshift.io/repositories/23680\" target=\"_blank\">\n\t\t<img src=\"https://trendshift.io/api/badge/repositories/23680\" alt=\"tinyhumansai%2Fopenhuman | Trendshift\" width=\"250\" height=\"55\"/>\n\t</a>\n\t<a href=\"https://www.producthunt.com/products/openhuman?embed=true&amp;utm_source=badge-top-post-badge&amp;utm_medium=badge&amp;utm_campaign=badge-openhuman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n\t\t<img alt=\"OpenHuman on Product Hunt\" width=\"250\" height=\"54\" src=\"https://api.producthunt.com/widgets/embed-image/v1/top-post-badge.svg?post_id=1136902&amp;theme=light&amp;period=daily&amp;t=1778916022823\">\n\t</a>\n\t<a href=\"https://www.producthunt.com/products/openhuman?embed=true&amp;utm_source=badge-top-post-badge&amp;utm_medium=badge&amp;utm_campaign=badge-openhuman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n\t\t<img alt=\"OpenHuman on Product Hunt\" width=\"250\" height=\"54\" src=\"https://api.producthunt.com/widgets/embed-image/v1/top-post-badge.svg?post_id=1136902&amp;theme=light&amp;period=weekly&amp;t=1779351403565\">\n\t</a>\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <a href=\"./README.md\">English</a> | <a href=\"./docs/README.ar.md\">العربية</a> | <a href=\"./docs/README.zh-CN.md\">简体中文</a> | <a href=\"./docs/README.ja-JP.md\">日本語</a> | <a href=\"./docs/README.ko.md\">한국어</a> | <a href=\"./docs/README.de.md\">Deutsch</a> | <a href=\"./docs/README.tr.md\">Türkçe</a> | <a href=\"./docs/README.ur-pk.md\">اردو</a>\n</p>\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> 🎉 Within one week of launch, OpenHuman became the **number one trending repository on GitHub** for nine days in a row.\n\n> **Early beta.** OpenHuman is under active development, so expect rough edges.\n\n---\n\n## Install\n\nThe easiest way is to download the desktop app from [tinyhumans.ai/openhuman](https://tinyhumans.ai/openhuman?utm_source=github&utm_medium=readme) or the [latest release](https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman/releases/latest). There is a `.dmg` for macOS, an `.msi` or `.exe` for Windows, and a `.deb` or `.AppImage` for Linux.\n\nPrefer the terminal? The install script picks the right package for your system, checks its checksum and installs it:\n\n```bash\n# macOS and Linux\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman/main/scripts/install.sh | bash\n```\n\n```powershell\n# Windows (PowerShell)\nirm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman/main/scripts/install.ps1 | iex\n```\n\nTo preview what the macOS and Linux script would do, end the command with `bash -s -- --dry-run`. Other options and troubleshooting are in [INSTALL.md](./INSTALL.md).\n\n---\n\n## Why OpenHuman?\n\nMost agent harnesses run one heavy process per agent and resend a big prompt on every call. OpenHuman does the same work with far less. It is the only feature-rich open-source harness built for large fleets of agents: 500 of them fit on a $10 server.\n\n<table>\n\n<tr>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>Fast</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman-benchmarks/blob/main/results/swe-x86-1/summary.md\">Benchmark results</a> · <a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/performance\">Cold-start numbers</a></p>\n\n<p>Finishes coding tasks in about 20 seconds, the fastest of seven AI agent tools we tested. Starts up in a tenth of a second.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>Cheap</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman-benchmarks/blob/main/results/swe-x86-1/summary.md\">Cost and token data</a> · <a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/token-compression\">How compression works</a></p>\n\n<p>Uses 2.6x fewer tokens than the typical agent tool, and had the lowest total bill in our test.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>Efficient at scale</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/performance\">Fleet measurements</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman-benchmarks/blob/main/profile/docs/library-benchmarking.md\">Methodology</a></p>\n\n<p>Uses about 8x less memory and CPU than the typical agent tool. Run more than 500 agents on a $10 server.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>Built for developers</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/quickstart\">Rust quickstart</a> · <a href=\"./docs/gitbooks/en/developing/embed/README.md\">Embedding guide</a> · <a href=\"./crates/openhuman-embed/examples\">Examples</a></p>\n\n<p>Use it as a Rust library: call an agent like any other function, or run a whole fleet from one small server.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n</tr>\n\n</table>\n\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n <img src=\"./docs/oh-v-hermes.gif\" alt=\"Hermes vs OpenHuman\" />\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\"><em>Same prompt, Same model, Same reasoning side by side: \"Write me a story about agent harnesses in the form of an anime story, write it into an HTML page and open it for me.\" OpenHuman finished in 20s, using 15k tokens for $0.0054. Hermes took 9min 40s, using 37k tokens for $0.0082.</em></p>\n\n---\n\n## Major innovations\n\nMost agent harnesses are a simple loop: send everything to the model, wait, repeat. That works for one agent, but it gets slow and expensive quickly, and it falls apart when you run hundreds.\n\nOpenHuman rethinks the parts that cost the most: how much text the AI has to read, how it finds the right tool, how features load, and how much machine the whole thing needs. The six ideas below are where the speed and savings above come from. Each card links to the docs if you want the details.\n\n<table>\n\n<tr>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>RLM token compression</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.24601\">RLM paper</a> · <a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/token-compression\">How it works</a></p>\n\n<p>Built on <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.24601\">Recursive Language Models</a>. Large tool results get compressed before the AI reads them. For very large ones, the AI gets a handle it can search instead of reading it all. Nothing is thrown away.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>Jev: instant, accurate tool search</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/jev\">Jev</a> · <a href=\"./docs/plans/jev-tool-search-baseline.md\">The measurements</a></p>\n\n<p>Jev is a tiny model that finds the right tool out of 1,215. The right one is in its top picks 86.8% of the time, against 70.5% for keyword search.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>Unified Rust bus</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/loadable-modules\">How plug-ins work</a></p>\n\n<p>Every feature, like search, documents or voice, plugs into one Rust bus, an idea borrowed from the <a href=\"https://packages.debian.org/stable/dbus\">Linux system bus</a>. A feature loads only when needed, and if one gets stuck, the rest keep working.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>Deeply integrated memory</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/memory\">How memory works</a> · <a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/engines\">Memory engines</a></p>\n\n<p>Memory comes built in. Before every turn, OpenHuman picks out only what matters, within a token budget, and hands it to the AI with citations. It works out of the box, and you can swap in a different memory engine with a setting.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>Instant browser and desktop control</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/native-tools/browser-and-computer\">Browser and computer control</a> · <a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/jev\">Jev</a></p>\n\n<p>The agent uses a real browser and your desktop apps. Jev picks each click from the buttons on screen, with no screenshots. It stops before any payment.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n<td width=\"50%\" valign=\"top\">\n\n<h3>A programmable Rust core</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/quickstart\">Rust quickstart</a> · <a href=\"https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/performance\">Performance</a></p>\n\n<p>The whole harness is compiled Rust in one process, so it starts in a tenth of a second and stays light: 68 MB at peak on our coding tasks. The same core is a library. Call an agent from your own Rust code, or run hundreds of them side by side on one small server.</p>\n\n</td>\n\n</tr>\n\n</table>\n\n---\n\n## Benchmarks\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n <picture>\n  <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"./docs/gitbooks/en/.gitbook/assets/benchmarks/swe-x86-1-dark.png\" />\n  <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"./docs/gitbooks/en/.gitbook/assets/benchmarks/swe-x86-1.png\" />\n  <img alt=\"SWE-bench Verified run swe-x86-1: OpenHuman against six other harnesses on ten panels\" src=\"./docs/gitbooks/en/.gitbook/assets/benchmarks/swe-x86-1.png\" />\n </picture>\n</p>\n\nWe ran seven harnesses on the same SWE-bench tasks, with the same model, API key and container, and metered every call. The setup and results are public in [openhuman-benchmarks](https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman-benchmarks), so anyone can rerun them. The latest run is [`swe-x86-1`](https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman-benchmarks/blob/main/results/swe-x86-1/summary.md), with ten tasks.\n\nOpenHuman finished its tasks in less than half the median time, using 2.6x fewer tokens and an eighth of the memory and CPU. It made 114 model calls where the others made 134 to 212, and its whole run cost $0.05 where the others cost $0.08 to $0.35.\n\nIt wins by doing less work per step. The core is compiled Rust in a single process, not Node or Python, so it uses little memory and almost no CPU between model calls. It sends the smallest prompt of the seven, 4.6k tokens with its 20 tools included, so each call is cheaper and comes back faster (1.65 s median, also the fastest). It also needs fewer calls to reach an answer, which is where most of the savings come from.\n\n| Per solved task | OpenHuman (vs. median) | Median of the other six | Best of the other six |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Wall time (p50) | **19.8 s** (2.3x faster) | 46.5 s | 28.4 s (OpenCode) |\n| Tokens | **167k** (2.6x fewer) | 435k | 370k (Codex) |\n| Cost | **$0.0077** (-36%) | $0.012 | $0.0077 (OpenCode, a tie) |\n| Peak memory | **68 MB** (7.7x less) | 523 MB | 123 MB (Codex) |\n| CPU time | **1.3 s** (8x less) | 10.4 s | 2.9 s (DeepSeek Harness) |\n| System prompt | **4.6k tokens** (-40%) | 7.7k | 6.2k (DeepSeek Harness) |\n\n---\n\n## For users\n\nIf you use Claude Code, Codex, OpenClaw or Hermes, you already know the ideas: an agent loop with tools, MCP, skills, BYOK models and memory. OpenHuman has all of them, in a desktop app, a terminal app or a headless server.\n\n| Capability | What OpenHuman ships |\n| --- | --- |\n| Configurable memory | [Built-in memory over your files, repos, feeds and apps](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/memory), recalled before every turn with citations, on the memory engine you choose |\n| Voice agents | A [live voice agent](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/native-tools/voice) you can interrupt mid-sentence, plus dictation and spoken replies |\n| Computer and browser control | [Drives a real Chrome browser and your desktop apps](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/native-tools/browser-and-computer), asking before anything it cannot undo |\n| Search | [Search engines and search agents](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/native-tools/web-search): Exa and Gemini included, Brave, Tavily, Parallel and more with your own key, grounded answers with citations and Deep Research |\n| Tools | [Native tools](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/native-tools): shell and coder, scraper, documents, image and video generation, cron |\n| MCP and skills | [MCP servers and skill bundles](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/integrations/mcp-and-skills) |\n| OAuth integrations | [119 apps through Composio](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/integrations), with [triggers](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/integrations/triggers) that start the agent when something happens |\n| Models | [Local models (Ollama, LM Studio, MLX) and BYOK for 26 providers](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/model-routing/local-and-byok-models), with [automatic model routing](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/model-routing) |\n| Channels | [14 messaging channels](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/channels) as agent front ends: Telegram, Discord, iMessage, email and more |\n| Workflows | [Durable workflow graphs](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/workflows): cron, event or manual triggers, approval steps, resume after a pause |\n| Safety | [Approval gate](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/approval-gate), [sandboxed execution](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/privacy-and-security) (OS jail or Docker), [Privacy Mode](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/privacy-mode) for local-only runs, secrets in the [OS keyring](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/os-keyring-and-secret-storage) |\n| Usage tracking | [Per-call cost and token usage](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/billing-and-usage), plus replayable run journals |\n\nStart with [Getting started](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/overview/getting-started) or the [guides](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/guides).\n\n---\n\n## For developers\n\nOpenHuman is a library-first harness. Add it to your Rust project and call an agent like any other function, with no sidecar or daemon to run. One runtime can hold hundreds of agents, each with its own model, tools, memory and sandbox. 500 of them fit on a $10 VPS, so a product with one agent per customer can start without a cluster.\n\n```rust\nuse openhuman_embed::{Access, Harness, Provider, Workspace};\n\nlet agent = Harness::builder()\n    .provider(Provider::openai_compatible(\"https://api.openai.com/v1\", \"sk-...\").model(\"gpt-5\"))\n    .workspace(Workspace::Ephemeral)\n    .access(Access::readonly())\n    .build()\n    .await?;\n\nlet reply = agent.run(\"Summarize what you can see in this directory.\").await?;\nprintln!(\"{}\", reply.reply);\n```\n\nNext: the [Rust quickstart](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/quickstart), the [embedding guide](./docs/gitbooks/en/developing/embed/README.md) and the [developer docs](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing).\n\n---\n\n## How it compares?\n\nHere is OpenHuman next to the harnesses most people already use. The top four rows come from our [public benchmark](https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman-benchmarks/blob/main/results/swe-x86-1/summary.md): ten SWE-bench coding tasks, with the same model and container for every harness. Each number is per solved task. The rest compare features.\n\nIn short, the others are great for one person working with one agent. OpenHuman does that too, and it is also the open-source option you can embed as a library and scale to a fleet. Products change quickly, so check each project before you decide.\n\n|                         | Claude Code          | Codex                | OpenClaw             | Hermes Agent         | OpenHuman                                |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| **Median task time**    | 37.8 s               | 36.1 s               | 62 s                 | 58.5 s               | 🚀 19.8 s                                 |\n| **Avg tokens per task** | 428k                 | 370k                 | 442k                 | 482k                 | 🚀 167k                                   |\n| **Peak memory per task** | 231 MB               | 123 MB               | 1.57 GB              | 816 MB               | 🚀 68 MB                                  |\n| **Avg cost per task**   | $0.04                | $0.02                | $0.01                | $0.0082              | 🚀 $0.0077                                |\n| **Open source**         | 🚫 Proprietary       | ✅ Apache-2.0        | ✅ MIT               | ✅ MIT               | ✅ GPL-3.0                               |\n| **Agent fleets**        | ⚠️ Process per agent | ⚠️ Process per agent | ⚠️ Process per agent | ⚠️ Process per agent | 🚀 500 agents on a $10 VPS               |\n| **Embeddable library**  | ⚠️ SDK over a CLI    | ⚠️ SDK over a CLI    | 🚫 None              | ⚠️ Python package    | 🚀 Typed Rust API                        |\n| **Memory**              | ⚠️ Memory files      | ⚠️ Memory files      | ⚠️ Plugin-reliant    | ✅ Self-learning     | 🚀 Recalled every turn, with citations   |\n| **Integrations**        | ✅ MCP               | ✅ MCP               | ⚠️ BYO               | ⚠️ BYO               | 🚀 119 OAuth apps, MCP, skills           |\n| **Messaging channels**  | 🚫 None              | 🚫 None              | ✅ Many              | ✅ Several           | ✅ 14, including email                   |\n| **Browser and desktop** | ⚠️ Browser via MCP   | ⚠️ Browser via MCP   | ✅ Browser           | ✅ Browser           | ✅ Browser and desktop apps              |\n| **Workflows**           | 🚫 None              | 🚫 None              | ⚠️ Scripts           | ⚠️ Scripts           | 🚀 Visual, agent-drafted                 |\n| **Model choice**        | ⚠️ Anthropic models  | ⚠️ OpenAI-first      | ✅ Any               | ✅ Any               | ✅ Any, with built-in routing            |\n\n---\n\n## Contributing\n\nRead [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](./CONTRIBUTING.md), or let an AI coding agent guide you with [this prompt](./docs/CONTRIBUTING-BEGINNERS.md#optional--let-an-ai-coding-agent-guide-you).\n\n1. Install Git, Node.js 24+, pnpm 10.10.0, Rust 1.99.1 (with `rustfmt` and `clippy`), CMake, Ninja, ripgrep, and your platform's desktop build prerequisites.\n2. Fork and clone the repo. Run `git submodule update --init --recursive`, then `pnpm install`.\n3. Run `pnpm dev` for UI work or `pnpm dev:app` for the desktop app. Before you open a PR, run `pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm format:check` and `cargo check --manifest-path Cargo.toml`.\n\nMore in [Getting set up](https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/getting-set-up), [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md) and the [crates overview](./crates/README.md). Many parts of OpenHuman live in their own repos under [`vendor/`](./vendor), and they welcome contributions too.\n\nContributors get free merch and special access on [Discord](https://guild.tinyhumans.ai/).\n\n## Star history\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n <a href=\"https://www.star-history.com/#tinyhumansai/openhuman&type=date&legend=top-left\">\n <picture>\n <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://api.star-history.com/svg?repos=tinyhumansai/openhuman&type=date&theme=dark&legend=top-left\" />\n <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://api.star-history.com/svg?repos=tinyhumansai/openhuman&type=date&legend=top-left\" />\n <img alt=\"Star History Chart\" src=\"https://api.star-history.com/svg?repos=tinyhumansai/openhuman&type=date&legend=top-left\" />\n </picture>\n </a>\n</p>\n\n## Contributors\n\n<a href=\"https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman/graphs/contributors\">\n <img src=\"https://contrib.rocks/image?repo=tinyhumansai/openhuman\" alt=\"OpenHuman contributors\" />\n</a>\n"
 },
