 "readme:rivet-dev/rivet": {
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T20:13:52.300Z",
  "bytes": 13408,
  "text": "<div align=\"center\">\n  <a href=\"https://www.rivet.dev\">\n    <picture>\n      <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"./.github/media/logo/icon-text-white.svg\" alt=\"Rivet\">\n      <img src=\"./.github/media/logo/icon-text-black.svg\" alt=\"Rivet\" height=\"75\">\n    </picture>\n  </a>\n  <br/>\n  <br/>\n  <h3>Fast, high-density, scalable orchestration with Rivet Actors.<br/>Built on S3 at SSD speeds.</h3>\n  <p>\n    4.99 ms p99 cold starts, 72 KB RAM per Actor, scales to billions\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <a href=\"https://www.rivet.dev/docs\">Quickstart</a> •\n    <a href=\"https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs\">Documentation</a> •\n    <a href=\"https://www.rivet.dev/changelog\">Changelog</a> •\n    <a href=\"https://www.rivet.dev/discord\">Discord</a> •\n    <a href=\"https://x.com/rivet_dev\">X</a>\n  </p>\n</div>\n\n## What is Rivet?\n\nRivet is an orchestrator for agentic workloads built on the actor model. Each Rivet Actor is a long-lived process with in-memory state, a SQLite database, WebSockets for realtime, and scheduling, addressed by key. Create one per agent, session, user, or tenant, and run it on plain Node.js, Bun, or Rust.\n\n- **72 KB per Actor**: Actors share a node process instead of each booting a sandbox, so one server holds hundreds of thousands.\n- **4.99 ms p99 cold starts**: No image pull and no VM boot.\n- **SQLite backed by S3, at SSD speeds**: 1.07 ms durable writes and 1.36 ms reads at p99, for about 10.4× less than replicated SSD storage.\n- **Hibernates when idle**: Idle Actors cost nothing and wake on the next request with nothing lost.\n\n**Backend**\n\n```typescript\nimport { actor } from \"rivetkit\";\nimport { db } from \"rivetkit/db\";\n\nconst agent = actor({\n  // Per-Actor SQLite database, persisted across restarts and hibernation\n  db: db({\n    onMigrate: async (db) => {\n      // Apply the SQLite schema\n      await db.execute(`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (role TEXT, content TEXT)`);\n    },\n  }),\n\n  actions: {\n    chat: async (c, text: string) => {\n      // Durably save the message\n      await c.db.execute(\"INSERT INTO messages VALUES (?, ?)\", \"user\", text);\n      const messages = await c.db.execute(\"SELECT role, content FROM messages\");\n\n      const response = streamText({ model: openai(\"gpt-5\"), messages });\n\n      // Stream tokens to every connected client for multiplayer\n      for await (const delta of response.textStream) {\n        c.broadcast(\"token\", delta);\n      }\n\n      // Durably save the response\n      await c.db.execute(\"INSERT INTO messages VALUES (?, ?)\", \"assistant\", await response.text);\n    },\n  },\n});\n```\n\n**Client** (frontend or backend)\n\n```typescript\n// Connect to an Actor\nconst agent = client.agent.getOrCreate(\"agent-123\").connect();\n\n// Listen for realtime events\nagent.on(\"token\", delta => process.stdout.write(delta));\n\n// Call an action on the Actor\nawait agent.chat(\"how many r's in strawberry?\");\n```\n\n## Getting Started\n\n### Use with your coding agent\n\nPaste this prompt into Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, or any coding agent:\n\n```text\nRun npx skills add rivet-dev/skills, then ask me what to build and run it locally.\n```\n\n### Start from scratch\n\nEvery product Rivet ships is an Actor, scheduled by the same control plane.\n\n- **[Actors](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs)**: Long-lived processes with state, realtime, and scheduling built in.\n- **[Agents](https://www.rivet.dev/agents/docs)**: A durable process per agent, with memory that survives restarts.\n- **[Workflows](https://www.rivet.dev/workflows/docs)**: Multi-step operations that replay instead of starting over.\n- **[agentOS](https://www.rivet.dev/agentos/docs)**: Run the code your agent writes, securely, with granular control over network access, filesystem access, and resource limits.\n- **[Dynamic Apps](https://www.rivet.dev/dynamic-apps/docs)** (preview): A backend per user, deployed the moment it is generated.\n\n## Built for everything agents need\n\n- **Coding agents**: One Actor per agent, with a durable process and state that survives restarts.\n- **Agent app builders**: Each generated backend deploys as its own Actor that scales to zero.\n- **Agents in your VPC**: Run agents next to your databases and APIs, so no data leaves your cloud.\n- **Realtime apps**: One Actor per document, room, or channel, broadcasting changes to every client.\n\n## How Actors compare to sandboxes\n\n| Metric | Rivet Actor | Sandbox |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Cold start (p99) | **4.99 ms** | 1.70 s |\n| Memory per instance | **72.4 KB** | 512 MiB |\n| Storage | **SQLite on S3** | Disk per sandbox |\n| Storage cost | **10.4× cheaper** | Replicated SSDs |\n\n<details>\n<summary>Benchmark details & methodology</summary>\n\n- **Rivet numbers** come from the published [Rivet Actors vs Cloudflare Durable Objects benchmark](https://github.com/rivet-dev/bench-rivet-actors-vs-durable-objects), run on Rivet 3.0.3. Its repository has the raw samples and the scripts to reproduce them.\n- **Sandbox numbers** are a documented default memory allocation and a third-party cold start benchmark.\n- **Storage cost** compares S3 Standard with three Amazon EBS gp3 copies at AWS list prices.\n- Sources, dates, and the full method are at [rivet.dev/docs/benchmarking](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/benchmarking/).\n\n</details>\n\n## Features\n\n- **Fault tolerance**: If a node goes down, its Actors move elsewhere with their state intact.\n- **HTTP & WebSocket networking**: Call an Actor over HTTP or hold a live WebSocket to it.\n- **End-to-end type safety**: Typed clients for your backend and React hooks for your frontend.\n- **Cron & scheduling**: Wake an Actor on a schedule or at a time it sets for itself.\n- **Actor-to-Actor calls**: Actors call each other by key across the cluster.\n- **OpenTelemetry**: Traces, metrics, and logs in OTel format.\n\n## Deployment Options\n\n| Option | Description | Get started |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Local & self-host** | Run Rivet locally while you build. In production, run the control plane on Docker, Kubernetes, or a VM. S3 is the only dependency. | [Self-hosting docs](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/control-plane/) |\n| **Rivet Cloud** | Fully managed, dedicated clusters on AWS or GCP in any region. Run your nodes on Rivet Cloud or bring your own. | [Dashboard](https://dashboard.rivet.dev) |\n| **BYOC** | The control plane runs inside your own VPC on AWS, GCP, or Azure, fully managed by Rivet. Your data never leaves your cloud. | [BYOC docs](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/byoc/) |\n\nEvery option runs the same control plane, from local dev to production. [Talk to an engineer →](https://www.rivet.dev/talk-to-an-engineer)\n\n## Works with the tools you already use\n\nTyped SDKs for your backend and React hooks for your frontend. Rivet runs on native Node.js and Bun with no custom runtime, so your existing packages, tooling, and tests work unchanged.\n\n**Deploy nodes on**: [Docker Compose](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/nodes/docker-compose/) • [Kubernetes](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/nodes/kubernetes/) • [VM & bare metal](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/nodes/vm/) • [Hetzner](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/nodes/hetzner/) • [AWS ECS](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/nodes/aws-ecs/) • [Railway](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/nodes/railway/) • [Render](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/nodes/render/)\n\n**Frameworks**: [React](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs/clients/react) • [Effect](./examples/ai-agent-effect)\n\n**Runtimes**: [Node.js](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs/quickstart/backend) • [Bun](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs/quickstart/backend) • [Rust](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs/quickstart/rust)\n\n**Tools**: [Vitest](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs/testing) • [OpenTelemetry](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs/general/tracing) • [Pino](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs/general/logging) • [AI SDK](./examples/ai-agent) • [OpenAPI](./rivetkit-openapi) • [AsyncAPI](./rivetkit-asyncapi)\n\n[Request an integration →](https://github.com/rivet-dev/rivet/issues/new)\n\n## FAQ\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>Does Rivet have BYOC?</b></summary>\n\nYes. Rivet deploys into your own cloud account, including air-gapped environments: the control plane and storage run inside your VPC while Rivet operates them. See the [BYOC docs](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/byoc/).\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>Does Rivet have a cloud?</b></summary>\n\nYes. [Rivet Cloud](https://dashboard.rivet.dev) runs fully managed, dedicated clusters on AWS or GCP in any region. It can run your app's Docker image as [nodes on Rivet Cloud](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/cloud/compute/), or you can [bring your own nodes](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/nodes/).\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>Can I self-host Rivet?</b></summary>\n\nYes. Rivet is fully self-hostable; see the [self-hosting docs](https://www.rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/control-plane/). The control plane runs anywhere a container runs, with guides for Docker Compose, Kubernetes, VMs and bare metal, Hetzner, Railway, Render, and AWS ECS. For enterprise support with a self-hosted deployment, [contact us](https://www.rivet.dev/enterprise/).\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>Do I still need Postgres, FoundationDB, or NATS?</b></summary>\n\nNo. An object storage bucket is the only dependency: there is no database to run and no message bus, and control plane instances find each other through the bucket. Rivet works with Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, Azure Blob Storage, and any S3-compatible store such as MinIO, Cloudflare R2, or Hetzner Object Storage. A local filesystem backend is available for trying Rivet on one machine.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>Is Rivet open-source?</b></summary>\n\nYes. Rivet is open source under the permissive Apache 2.0 license. You're looking at it.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>How does Rivet compare to sandboxes?</b></summary>\n\nA sandbox boots a VM or container per agent, which is slow to start and sits mostly idle. A Rivet Actor runs inside a shared node process: 72 KB and 4.99 ms p99 to start, versus 512 MiB and 1.70 s for a sandbox, and it hibernates when idle. To run untrusted code your agent writes, use [agentOS](https://www.rivet.dev/agentos/docs).\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>How does Rivet compare to Cloudflare Durable Objects?</b></summary>\n\nRivet is often used as an open-source alternative to Cloudflare Durable Objects that is easy to self-host and scales. It also fixes many of the constraints of Durable Objects: no 128 MB memory cap, bottomless storage instead of a 10 GB SQLite limit, Actors are not randomly evicted, and you control the runtime because Actors run on vanilla Node.js or Bun instead of a custom runtime. Rivet also adds built-in connection handling, event broadcasting, durable scheduling and cron, full Vitest support, lifecycle hooks for draining, and control over Actor upgrades. See the [Cloudflare Durable Objects comparison](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/compare/rivet-actors-vs-cloudflare-durable-objects/).\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>Does Rivet use a custom V8 runtime?</b></summary>\n\nNo. Rivet runs on native Node.js and Bun, so your existing packages, tooling, and tests work unchanged.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>Is Rivet like Erlang, Akka, or Orleans?</b></summary>\n\nRivet implements the virtual actor pattern, which is closest to Orleans, but focuses on what modern workloads need: SQLite persistence instead of simple JSON stores and support for modern runtimes. Erlang and Akka do not provide virtual actors out of the box, though ecosystem packages exist that offer functionality comparable to Rivet Actors.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><b>Does Rivet require microVMs, gVisor, or nested virtualization?</b></summary>\n\nNo. Rivet only needs a standard process that connects to the control plane over WebSocket. That process can be Node.js, Bun, Rust, or anything else.\n\n</details>\n\n## Projects in This Repository\n\n| Project | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| [RivetKit TypeScript](./rivetkit-typescript) | Client & server library for building Actors |\n| [RivetKit Rust](./rivetkit-rust) | Rust SDK |\n| [RivetKit Python](./rivetkit-python) | Python client (experimental) |\n| [RivetKit Swift](./rivetkit-swift) | Swift client (experimental) |\n| [Control Plane](./engine) | Rust control plane that schedules, routes, and persists Actors |\n| ↳ [Pegboard](./engine/packages/pegboard) | Actor scheduling & networking |\n| ↳ [Gasoline](./engine/packages/gasoline) | Durable execution engine |\n| ↳ [Guard](./engine/packages/guard) | Traffic routing proxy |\n| ↳ [Epoxy](./engine/packages/epoxy) | Multi-region KV store (EPaxos) |\n| [Dashboard](./frontend) | Inspector for debugging Actors |\n| [Documentation](./docs) | Source for [rivet.dev/actors/docs](https://www.rivet.dev/actors/docs) |\n| [Examples](./examples) | Runnable examples |\n\n## Community\n\n- [Discord](https://www.rivet.dev/discord) - Chat with the community\n- [X/Twitter](https://x.com/rivet_dev) - Follow for updates\n- [Bluesky](https://bsky.app/profile/www.rivet.dev) - Follow for updates\n- [GitHub Discussions](https://github.com/rivet-dev/rivet/discussions) - Ask questions\n- [GitHub Issues](https://github.com/rivet-dev/rivet/issues) - Report bugs\n- [Talk to an engineer](https://www.rivet.dev/talk-to-an-engineer) - Discuss your use case\n\n## License\n\n[Apache 2.0](LICENSE)\n"
 },
 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50381687": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T20:13:53.504Z",
  "bytes": 14126,
  "text": " Launch HN: Rivet (YC W23) – Scale and Orchestrate Agents on S3 | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login Launch HN: Rivet (YC W23) – Scale and Orchestrate Agents on S3 ( github.com/rivet-dev ) 42 points by NathanFlurry 2 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 23 comments Hey HN, this is Nathan and Nicholas from Rivet ( https://rivet.dev ) – a fast, high-density, and scalable orchestrator for agentic workloads using the actor model. Rather than running a heavyweight sandbox per agent, each agent gets a lightweight actor backed by S3. Actors provide a long-lived process for long agent turns, in-memory state for context, a WebSocket server for multiplayer, durable SQLite storage backed by S3, and hibernation when idle. It's a similar model to Cloudflare Durable Objects, but it’s open source and self-hostable. Here’s a video walkthrough: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3Ev6I7DOV9c When we went through YC in 2023, we were building infrastructure for multiplayer games like Diep and Krunker. This involved heavily optimizing high-throughput matchmaking, real-time player messages, and blitz scaling. Through that process, we spent a lot of time on workloads where Postgres couldn't keep up with our throughput. We started out by hacking together Lua scripts on top of Redis, but this became too cumbersome to maintain and upgrade. This led us to extend our game server orchestrator to support what we call Rivet Actors today: many lightweight workers with in-memory state and durable storage. By using actors, we were able to serve tens of millions of monthly active users. As agents grew in popularity, it became clear that running agents in sandboxes is inefficient. Sandboxes are expensive, mostly idle, and slow to start. Developers started using actors because they are far more memory efficient (72 KB baseline for actors vs 512 MB for a minimal sandbox) and fast to start (4.9 ms p99 for actors vs 1.7 s p99 for sandboxes). For example, Amp is using Rivet to orchestrate multiplayer agents. They originally tried to build on Cloudflare Durable Objects, but couldn’t launch because of the platform’s limitations and unresponsive support. They migrated to Rivet Actors in a weekend and deploy on-prem. Rivet’s flexible architecture lets them adapt it to their own use cases without being constrained by a framework or the memory, storage, and runtime limits of Cloudflare Durable Objects. Under the hood, Rivet is backed by S3 while maintaining SSD speeds. Why S3? Previously, Rivet ran on FoundationDB. However, we were surprised to find that companies are storing orders of magnitude more data on Rivet Actors than in Postgres, such as large session transcripts, agent artifacts, and full POSIX filesystems. As their data grew, they had to start paying for SSDs that sat mostly idle with old transcripts. This led us to double down on S3 as our primary storage medium without sacrificing performance. This choice means that data stored on Rivet is 10× cheaper than on EBS gp3 while maintaining SSD speeds. It’s not as simple as writing data straight to S3. Lots of PUTs for tiny blobs of data are slow and expensive. Instead, we use an SSD tier to optimize speed and cost: When you write data to an actor’s SQLite database, the write is acknowledged once SSDs in 2 of 3 availability zones have it. Then, the data is batched to S3 in the background. This means that S3 is never on the write path, which lowers latency and cost. When reading data or waking an actor, Rivet reads from SSDs if already cached and falls back to S3 if not. SSD caches can be tuned to meet your needs, giving you configurable tiered storage for your actors. This architecture lets Rivet provide 1.07 ms durable writes (3 AZ, p99), 1.36 ms reads (p99), and 138 ms reads from object storage (p99). We’ve spent a lot of time making sure that Rivet can run wherever you need it. Self-hosting requires deploying a single Rust binary with S3 as the only dependency, Rivet Cloud lets you deploy dedicated clusters on AWS or GCP in any region, and BYOC lets us deploy Rivet to your VPC without having to operate it yourself. Rivet also runs identically in local dev as in production, since Rivet is a lightweight binary that runs anywhere Rivet Actors can be written in TypeScript or Rust. Out of the box, they support agents (Pi Durable), workflows, real-time, multiplayer, running AI-generated code, Durable Streams, and integrations with tools like Vercel’s Eve and Turso. See all of our available guides, or point your agent at them: https://rivet.dev/docs/#guides Try it out now, by reading our quickstart guides ( https://rivet.dev/docs/ ) or ask your agent: “Run npx skills add rivet-dev/skills, then build a multiplayer chat agent and run it locally.” We’d love to get feedback on your experience with Rivet! We’re deeply invested in making Rivet an easy-to-self-host, flexible, and efficient way to run agentic workloads. GitHub: https://github.com/rivet-dev/rivet Architecture: https://rivet.dev/docs/architecture/ Benchmarks: https://rivet.dev/docs/benchmarking/ Self-hosting: https://rivet.dev/docs/deploy/self-host/ help \n \n SriniK 5 minutes ago | next [–] \n Congrats! How is the security posture? Without really comparing isolation and sandboxing etc.. comparing with E2B is not the same. reply dragonkhoi 8 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n Been running Rivet v3 self-hosted on AWS and it's dope! reply fastball 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Rivet is very cool. Switched a Cloudflare Durable Object piece of our infrastructure that lived in its own little fairy tale land to our own infra within the rest of our VPC. Was a very pleasant experience. Haven't had the chance to try Rivet v3 yet but hoping to get to it soon. reply NathanFlurry 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Appreciate the kind words, would love to hear feedback on v3 in our Discord when you upgrade! reply keeganpoppen 20 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n fairy tale land? reply haseebws94 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n Hey Nathan! I remember Rivet from Playflow, back when you guys did game server orchestration. Great to see you guys grow! Have you had agents like claude code , codex etc all fully running in agent-os? Trying to understand the use case a bit better here. For context, I've recently built our own pipeline which spins up ephemeral linux vms for claude code, codex, etc which do their work & the state always persists but frees up resources when finished. Is the idea pretty much similar for agent-os here? Also with the s3 backing changes, it reminds me a lot of the work done on celld, good to see more self-hostable durable objects out there. reply NathanFlurry 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n Been a minute since the gaming days, thanks for reaching out! > Have you had agents like claude code , codex etc all fully running in agent-os? We have gotten them all working inside of agentOS. However, we found it's a better architecture to run the agent loop outside of agentOS and expose it as tools. The trust boundary & blast radius of running the agent inside the sandbox makes it difficult. Also, the memory overhead of running individual Claude Code instances is nuts, it's better to run the agents in a single process using tools. I wrote a bit more on it here: https://rivet.dev/blog/2026-07-27-run-your-harness-outside-t... > Also with the s3 backing changes, it reminds me a lot of the work done on celld, good to see more self-hostable durable objects out there. Appreciate it! Rivet has a much different architecture than most S3-backed databases. Having an object-per-actor doesn't scale well with cost and performance, so we built a SSD tiered storage engine instead: https://rivet.dev/changelog/2026-10-26-introducing-rivet-3-0... Ping me on Discord if you give it a try, would be fun to catch up: https://rivet.dev/discord reply apsurd 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Wouldn't an agent in any capacity need a sandbox runtime to do agentic work? When you say \"sandboxes are too slow\" do you mean needing to boot the sandbox to get back Agent state? Is the idea that state/memory/context and runtime env are separate concepts in rivet? (I'm a professional developer but don't do any orchestration so trying to understand from basic mental model pov) reply k9294 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n There is a notion right now of decoupling the agent loop from the sandbox, so the sandbox isn't waiting for request-response from the agent. This would probably be 90+% of all the agent work. If you combine this with products like Sprites from Fly.io (or OSS version - agent substrate), where you pay only per execution request in a sandbox, you can have huge savings compare to the event loop inside the sanbdox. You will be billed only for request executions (tens of milliseconds per read/write, hundreds of milliseconds per search, etc.) and zero for all the waiting time during the the inference phase. Also, if you don't require a real Linux sandbox — you might actually emulate the environment, e.g. just-bash idea with linux-like environment to give agents something that looks like their native habitat, but under the hood, it can be just CF Durable Objects SQLite or whatever else. Probably(?) almost all knowledge work doesn't require a Linux sandbox. You can just build a document reading and editing agent that does tool calls and connects to other products via MCPs, APIs, etc. And lightweight actors make a lot of sense to me in these cases. reply NathanFlurry 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Exactly this. > Also, if you don't require a real Linux sandbox — you might actually emulate the environment We built agentOS for this: https://rivet.dev/agentos/ It provides Linux-compatible sandboxing using V8 / WebAssembly. Supports full POSIX filesystems, networking stacks, and process trees. reply apsurd 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n This is a very helpful explanation, thank you. The part about basically duck-typing an Agent's environment and tool access is particularly insightful. reply NathanFlurry 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Hey! > Wouldn't an agent in any capacity need a sandbox runtime to do agentic work? Developers on Rivet use 3 different patterns: - Just agents: many agents just need tools, such as customer support or personal agents - Agents with lightweight sandbox: Rivet provides a V8 / WebAssembly sandboxing library for running JS & Python with a filesystem, which covers most workloads like data analysis, file processing, evals, and code mode ( https://rivet.dev/agentos/ ) - Agent outside of the sandbox: expose the sandbox to the agent as tools, which is better for blast radius, trust boundary, and orchestration (see below) > Is the idea that state/memory/context and runtime env are separate concepts in rivet? Exactly, Rivet Actors support running the harness outside of the sandbox. It's often referred to \"separation of head from the hands.\" I wrote a bit about why a while back: https://rivet.dev/blog/2026-07-27-run-your-harness-outside-t... > When you say \"sandboxes are too slow\" do you mean needing to boot the sandbox to get back Agent state? Specifically coldstarts. As LLMs are getting faster and have more realtime use cases, sandboxes are become the bottleneck on time to interactive for an agent. reply stiiv 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n What if I just want self-hostable TypeScript actors _without agents_? I'm a long-time fan of Akka et. al., but to my knowledge Node never got a proper framework. reply NathanFlurry 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n Hey! Rivet is pure actors at its core, there's nothing inherently specific to agents. Instead, we provide an optional agent library that runs Pi Durable on top of actors ( https://rivet.dev/agents/docs/quickstart/ ) or you can write your agent from scratch if you choose. reply esafak 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n My first reaction: this is weird; agentic workloads have many problems but orchestration speed ain't one of them. You spend most of the time waiting for the model! Is this for small models? I would have lead with the durability aspect. reply feifan 19 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n this is for people/products that run dozens/hundreds of agents concurrently. reply minraws 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Interesting idea, horrible ux atm though, my codex/claude whatever is just as confused as me what and how are we supposed to use it exactly and all the edge cases. Hope you folks can figure out some interesting ideas to improve it though. reply NathanFlurry 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n Hey! Can you share a bit more about what your agent is saying so we can fix this? Would love to chat with you in Discord too: https://rivet.dev/discord reply chicagobuss 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n looks cool - I'm curious how this is similar / different from celld ..and how you're doing anything with s3-backing durably and safely with latency numbers less than several hundred ms reply Tanjreeve 4 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n In OP > It’s not as simple as writing data straight to S3. Lots of PUTs for tiny blobs of data are slow and expensive. Instead, we use an SSD tier to optimize speed and cost: > When you write data to an actor’s SQLite database, the write is acknowledged once SSDs in 2 of 3 availability zones have it. Then, the data is batched to S3 in the background. This means that S3 is never on the write path, which lowers latency and cost. When reading data or waking an actor, Rivet reads from SSDs if already cached and falls back to S3 if not. SSD caches can be tuned to meet your needs, giving you configurable tiered storage for your actors. reply bigbuppo 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n Ooh, so mod_rivet is getting funding? That's great. Tcl does not get enough love these days. reply realty_geek 2 hours ago | prev [–] \n Ah, another rivet. I initially thought it was a pivot of: https://rivet.ironcladapp.com/ reply NathanFlurry 2 hours ago | parent [–] \n <insert spiderman meme here> reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
