  "text": "<div align=\"center\">\n\n# A Text-to-Image Model Trained for $21K<br>(Pyramid-JiT)\n\n**[Sahil Chopra](https://thesahilchopra.com/) · [Manu Chopra](https://www.linkedin.com/in/manu-chopra-50360b170/)**\n\n*Linum*\n\n[![Blog post](https://img.shields.io/badge/blog%20post-linum.ai%2Ffield--notes%2Fpjit--21k-6b4fd8)](https://www.linum.ai/field-notes/pjit-21k)\n[![Weights](https://img.shields.io/badge/%F0%9F%A4%97%20weights-Linum--AI%2Fpjit--21k-ffcc4d)](https://huggingface.co/Linum-AI/pjit-21k)\n[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-Apache--2.0-blue)](LICENSE)\n\n</div>\n\n![Five P-JiT 21K samples: a close-up portrait of an elderly woman in a cream headscarf, a man blowing out birthday candles, a children's book illustration of a bear sharing honey with a rabbit, a giant panda in a bamboo grove, and an alpine meadow of wildflowers](assets/samples.jpg)\n\n*Five samples from the [blog post](https://www.linum.ai/field-notes/pjit-21k)'s gallery, at the\nmodel's native ~1 MP sizes. Their prompts and seeds are [below](#reproducing-the-samples).*\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Research checkpoint: this is an experimental research artifact, not a production model.**\n> P-JiT 21K is a 2B-parameter model trained on 660M samples for about $21K of compute, as a\n> stepping stone towards our next video model. We're releasing it because the results\n> surprised us for a model this size, and to encourage others to explore efficient training\n> methods like ours. You should expect rough edges.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **This model cannot write text or generate posters.** It was trained on little to no images\n> of rendered text: no infographics, PDFs, pages of books, slides or posters. We filtered that\n> data out on purpose (it isn't useful for our goal of animation tools), so prompts that ask\n> for words, signs, logos, title cards or posters will not work. The prompt upsampler returns a\n> `text_requested` error for these prompts instead of a caption.\n\nP-JiT 21K is the [P-JiT](https://www.linum.ai/field-notes/pyramid-jit) architecture trained for longer:\n610M samples at 512px, 50M at 1024px, then post-training with a variant of\n[Finite Difference Flow Optimization (FDFO)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12893). The\n[blog post](https://www.linum.ai/field-notes/pjit-21k) covers the training run.\n\n## Getting started\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install -y build-essential python3-dev python3-venv   # Triton compiles small CUDA shims at first use\ngit clone https://github.com/Linum-AI/pjit-21k && cd pjit-21k\npython3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install -e .\n```\n\nGenerate the portrait at the top left (no API key needed: the prompt is already a detailed\ncaption, so it skips the upsampler):\n\n```bash\nPROMPT=\"Close-up portrait of an elderly Middle Eastern woman looking calmly into the lens, her face filling the tall frame. A cream headscarf and the collar of a dark green cardigan frame her chin. Soft overcast daylight smooths the fine lines of her skin, and the olive grove behind melts into silvery blur.\"\npython generate.py --no_upsample --aspect_ratio tall --seeds 2 --prompt \"$PROMPT\" --out_dir outputs\n# writes outputs/seed_2.png\n```\n\nThe weights and the Qwen3.5-4B text encoder download from the Hugging Face Hub on first use.\nThe model needs a CUDA GPU: its weights are 8.7 GB in fp32 and the text encoder 8 GB in bf16,\nplus activations.\n\n### Your own prompts\n\nThe model was trained on dense captions of 50 to 80 words, and short prompts give noticeably\nworse images. So by default `generate.py` first rewrites your prompt into a caption of that\nkind with Claude Opus 5.5 through [OpenRouter](https://openrouter.ai), which needs an\nOpenRouter API key ([get one here](https://openrouter.ai/keys)):\n\n```bash\nexport OPENROUTER_API_KEY=...\npython generate.py --prompt \"oil painting of weathered fisherman smoking a pipe\" \\\n    --aspect_ratio square --seeds 0 --out_dir outputs\n```\n\nThe upsampler turns that prompt into a caption like this one (Claude writes a slightly\ndifferent one each time):\n\n> An oil painting of a weathered fisherman in his seventies in a close head-and-shoulders\n> portrait, a curved wooden pipe held in the corner of his mouth and a thin curl of smoke rising\n> beside his face. His skin is deeply lined and ruddy, his white beard rough, and he wears a dark\n> knitted cap and a yellow oilskin jacket. Warm lamplight falls on one cheek, laid on in thick,\n> textured brushstrokes against a muted blue-grey background.\n\nThe caption the model actually saw is printed and saved to `outputs/prompt.json` beside the\nPNGs. Pass `--no_upsample` to send a caption you've written yourself as is (no key needed).\n\n### Aspect ratios\n\n`--aspect_ratio` picks one of the five ~1 MP sizes the model was trained at:\n\n| `--aspect_ratio` | Width x height |\n|---|---|\n| `square` (default) | 1024 x 1024 |\n| `tall` | 864 x 1216 |\n| `4:3` | 1152 x 864 |\n| `3:2` | 1248 x 832 |\n| `16:9` | 1280 x 720 |\n\n`--width` and `--height` set any other size (sides that aren't a multiple of 32 are padded and\ncropped back), but the model has only seen these five.\n\n## Reproducing the samples\n\nEach image above is one seed, sampled with the default settings, no upsampling (the prompts are\nalready captions) and the initial noise drawn as on an H100 SXM. The portrait is the\n[Getting started](#getting-started) command; the other four are below. The gallery images were\nsampled five seeds to a batch, and the batch size changes how the GPU rounds its matrix\nmultiplies, so one seed on its own gives the same image with pixel values that differ by\nrounding (under 1/255 on average).\n\n**Birthday** (seed 0):\n\n```bash\nPROMPT=\"Cheeks puffed, a white man in his sixties leans over a birthday cake on a kitchen table and blows out a ring of thin candles, the flames bending away from him. A checked flannel shirt covers his grey t-shirt, his grey hair neatly combed. The cake has plain white frosting and fresh strawberries, and medium-format film keeps the colours warm and natural in the centred square composition.\"\npython generate.py --no_upsample --aspect_ratio square --seeds 0 --prompt \"$PROMPT\"\n```\n\n**Bear and rabbit** (seed 4):\n\n```bash\nPROMPT=\"A children's book illustration of a big brown bear sharing a jar of honey with a small white rabbit, the bear holding the jar out toward the rabbit as they sit together under a tree, drawn in soft colored pencil with warm, gentle colors. The pair sits in the center of the frame, seen from the front at eye level, with soft, grainy pencil strokes and a pale, simply sketched background that keeps the focus on their friendly exchange.\"\npython generate.py --no_upsample --aspect_ratio square --seeds 4 --prompt \"$PROMPT\"\n```\n\n**Panda** (seed 1):\n\n```bash\nPROMPT=\"Golden-hour light slants through a bamboo grove onto a giant panda sitting on mossy ground, a wide cinematic view with the bear placed left of centre. It grips a green stalk in both front paws and chews, its black-and-white fur glowing at the edges where the low sun catches it. Tall bamboo stems stretch away to the right, their leaves lit amber, and soft haze fills the gaps between them.\"\npython generate.py --no_upsample --aspect_ratio 16:9 --seeds 1 --prompt \"$PROMPT\"\n```\n\n**Meadow** (seed 1):\n\n```bash\nPROMPT=\"An alpine meadow sweeps up toward jagged grey peaks, thick with wildflowers: purple lupines, yellow arnica, white daisies and pink clover crowding the foreground. Soft overcast light keeps the colours rich and even, and low cloud drapes the higher ridges in pale wisps. A thin stream winds through the grass from the lower right, its banks lined with moss, and a scattering of dark spruce trees marks the far edge of the meadow where the slope turns to scree.\"\npython generate.py --no_upsample --aspect_ratio 3:2 --seeds 1 --prompt \"$PROMPT\"\n```\n\n## Limitations\n\nThis is a small model (a 2B-parameter DiT with a 4B text encoder), and there are areas where it\nstruggles:\n\n- Full-body shots\n- Many entities (six cars, crowds of people)\n- Interactions between several people\n- World knowledge (six strings on a guitar, the dots on a die)\n\n## Architecture\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Input patch | 32x32 px, linear 3,072 -> 1,024 -> 2,944 (1,024 tokens at 1024²) |\n| Trunk | 22 single-stream DiT blocks · width 2,944 · 23 heads x 128 · SwiGLU 7,936 |\n| Refiners | 2 image + 2 text blocks before the trunk, timestep-modulated |\n| Block | Sandwich RMSNorm, low-rank AdaLN (scale-in, tanh gate-out), q/k RMSNorm, sigmoid attention gate |\n| Position | 3-D RoPE on image tokens; height and width positions x 0.5 at 1024px (Position Interpolation from the 512px stage) |\n| Text | Qwen3.5-4B (frozen), hidden layers 7, 15, 27 concatenated -> MLP · 256 tokens |\n| Parameters | 2.18B |\n\nThe training-time readout heads and the PixelREPA adapter play no part in sampling and are not\nincluded. The [pyramid-jit](https://github.com/Linum-AI/pyramid-jit) repository has reference\nimplementations of them and of the rest of the pretraining objective.\n\n## Sampling\n\n50 Euler steps from noise (scale 4, the 1024px noise level) to the image, with\n[adaptive projected guidance](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02416) at scale 15 against an empty\nnegative prompt, under bf16 autocast. The state is carried in fp32 and the model sees it\nrounded to bf16, as in the sampler the release images were made with.\n\nThe initial noise is drawn exactly as `torch.randn` draws it on an H100 SXM, the GPU the model\nwas trained on ([`pjit_21k/noise.py`](pjit_21k/noise.py)), so a seed gives the same image on\nany NVIDIA GPU rather than one that depends on the GPU model. Pass `--native_noise` to use the\nlocal GPU's own draw. Attention runs on FlashAttention-3 when `flash_attn_interface` is\ninstalled and on PyTorch SDPA otherwise.\n\n## Citation\n\n```bibtex\n@online{chopra2026pjit21k,\n  title = {Training a Text-to-Image Model for \\$21K},\n  author = {Chopra, Sahil and Chopra, Manu},\n  year = {2026},\n  url = {https://www.linum.ai/field-notes/pjit-21k}\n}\n```\n\n## Authorship\n\nThis repository was written by Claude (Anthropic's Opus 5.5 model, running in Claude Code).\nLinum asked it to extract the model and inference code from Linum's internal experiment\nrepository and to add the prompt upsampler. The model, the training, and the review of this\nrepository are Linum's.\n\n## About Linum\n\nLinum is a team of two brothers building a tiny-yet-powerful AI research lab. We train our\nown generative media models from scratch.\n\n**Subscribe to [Field Notes](https://buttondown.com/linum-ai)**: technical deep dives on\nbuilding generative video models from the ground up, plus updates on new releases from Linum.\n\n**Contact:** hello@linum.ai\n\n## License\n\nApache-2.0. Copyright 2026 Linum Inc. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).\n"
 },
 "page:https://www.linum.ai/field-notes/pjit-21k": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T20:13:58.155Z",
  "bytes": 13580,
  "text": " Training a Text-to-Image Model for $21K | Field Notes by Linum Introduction Pressure testing the Pyramid-JiT (P-JiT) Training Regime Model Limitations Appendix A – Training Settings Introduction Contents × Introduction Pressure testing the Pyramid-JiT (P-JiT) Training Regime Model Limitations Appendix A – Training Settings Home · Field Notes Research · 10 min read Training a Text-to-Image Model for $21K Sahil Chopra & Manu Chopra Co-CEOs @ Linum · November 4, 2026 RSS Subscribe Road to Linum v3 · Issue 04 previously: P-JiT In our last blog post, we iterated towards P-JiT, a pixel-space architecture that cut attention context windows by 4× while converging in 11× fewer samples. Here, we train the P-JiT for 660M samples (just $21K in compute) as a stepping stone towards larger, video models. Research release · Apache 2.0 Model code Model weights \n 249 samples · 2B Pyramid-JiT Click any picture for full resolution and its prompt All 249 Person 57 Animal 42 Building 20 Product 43 Landscape 19 Stylized 68 \n Pressure testing the Pyramid-JiT (P-JiT) \n For us, this $21K training run is a proof-of-concept along the way to our next video model. We were surprised by how good the results looked given the size of the model, so we thought we'd share it with the world as an experimental release. \n If you haven't followed our work previously, we'd love to take a step back and introduce ourselves. We're a 2-person research lab training our own generative video models entirely from scratch. The ultimate ambition is creative tools that democratize access to animation. You can read more about that here . \n Today, generative video models have two major flaws: they are too slow/expensive and they lack proper controls. Creativity is an iterative process. You make, you tweak, you get better. With this double whammy, video models (as they stand today) are effectively useless for storytelling. \n When we set out to build Linum v3 earlier this year, the primary goal was to bring the cost envelope of inference down significantly. If our model is fast and cheap, it's the first step (of many) towards useful creative tools. \n We've written extensively about the steps we've taken in this direction, but here's the long story short: \n \n The bottleneck for training and inference for video is attention, O(n²). If we can compress more aggressively, we'll reduce the context window for attention, slashing the cost of training and inference. \n Typically, compression is handled by a separate specialized model like a Variational Autoencoder, but that's not necessarily optimal. VAEs seem to have an empirical ceiling on the amount of compression they can achieve. There are other ways to get more compression, but they require substantial surgery to the traditional VAE (e.g. the 2B decoder in Minimax's H3). More importantly, improving the VAE on its task of reconstruction can produce worse generations in the downstream diffusion model. \n If you get rid of the VAE altogether, you should be able to compress more aggressively and learn faster. Diffusion models are a more powerful family of generative models than VAEs. We should be able to use them to compress and generate at the same time. Instead of using another model's latent space, the diffusion model should learn a latent space purpose-built for its use case. In turn, we should be able to squeeze more juice out of this compression. \n \n In our last blog, we iterated towards a new architecture that we called the Pyramid-JiT (P-JiT). It's a pixel-space model that converges in 11× fewer samples than Linum v2 with 4× more aggressive token reduction (i.e., we represent a 512² image in the same number of tokens we used to represent a 256² image). Linum v2 is a fairly standard Wan VAE + DiT diffusion model. Linum v2 used a 16×16 token reduction like most models (e.g. FLUX-2). P-JiT enables 32×32 token reduction. This implies a 4× reduction in attention context-window. \n For the details on how we got there, we'd recommend you take a look at our previous blogs on JiT-DDT and P-JiT . But at a high level, we found a noise schedule for pixel-space models that helps the models better learn fine-grained detail. And, we added readouts along the trunk of the DiT. \n Adjacent work in REPA , RAE-v2 , and Self-Flow all point to weak gradients in the early blocks of the DiT. By operating in pixel space, every block of our DiT is quite literally predicting a version of the final image, so we can boost gradient signal earlier in the network by predicting low-resolution versions of the final image. This helps accelerate convergence. \n The architecture did well in our initial experiments, but we wanted to see how it performed when we trained it for longer (i.e., ~600M samples instead of 50–100M samples). For context, generative video models pretty much always start as generative image models. You have to learn nouns (people, places, things) before you can learn verbs (actions, movement). It's a pressure test of sorts along the way towards larger models (and video). \n Training Regime \n Overview of Pyramid-JiT (P-JiT) Architecture replay sizes read 512² stage / 1024² stage · flower = an illustrative target 2.18B params at sampling · purple heads are training-only and add ~0.24B, for ~2.42B in training \n Text and image each pass through two single-modality \"refiner\" DiT blocks, then merge into one stream of 22 identical DiT blocks. The text representation comes from a frozen Qwen3.5-4B model, which we read at layers 7, 15 and 27 (its three full-attention layers). We concatenate and project these representations into the text refiner. The image is split into 32×32 patches and compressed into a 1024-dimensional space, before being projected up into the image refiner blocks. This is a 3:1 compression rate. A 32×32 patch with RGB values is a 32×32×3 = 3,072 representation, which gets projected into 1024 dimensions. \n At block 5, we have a 2-layer PixelREPA readout that aligns the model's latent space to DINOv3-L. We mask 20% of the tokens in our hidden state and push it through a 2-layer transformer to predict DINOv3-L's feature representation of the same image. We have two linear readout heads along the trunk after blocks 10 and 16. As discussed earlier, these predict the clean image at a quarter and half resolution, respectively. The total loss is given by MSE at the readouts, PixelREPA cosine-distance, MSE at the final output head, and perceptual losses on the full resolution image ( LPIPS and P-DINO ). \n One DiT block: 22× in the trunk, 2× in each refiner blue = AdaLN · s scales the input before attention/FFN, g gates its residual write hidden size 2,944 · 23 heads × 128 · FFN inner width 5,376 in the trunk, 2,816 in the refiners \n Every DiT block is conditioned on the timestep through a low-rank AdaLN, following Z-Image . The timestep embedding goes through one down-projection to 256-dim. The image refiner and trunk share one down-projection; the text refiner has its own. Each block then has its own up-projection to a scale and a gate, zero-initialized so every block starts as the identity. \n As described in our last blog, we employ a curriculum over the noise schedule. We start concentrated at high noise for the first 80M samples, so the model learns basic structure. Then, we widen the noise distribution for the remaining training time so it can learn fine-grained details. See Appendix A for details. \n We train the model in several stages as we go from 512² to 1024² replay H100-hours 512² · 0 1024² · 0 0 Samples seen 512² · 0M 1024² · 0M 0M Validation loss on a fixed set of 20,480 square images at each stage's resolution. The live spike to 0.157 at 80M is the first eval after the timestep schedule widens. The x-axis widens after 550M so the cooldown and the 1024² stage are legible; the bars are to scale. \n We start EMA at 100M samples, so the model has already learned decent representations of structure and detail. When we switch to 1024², we start with a fresh optimizer and resume training from the 512² EMA weights. At 1024² we double the noise scale from 2 to 4 to account for the redundancy of information (see JiT, Appendix A ). The higher noise level and need to reproduce fine detail exactly at high resolution result in a higher overall loss, even as image quality improves rapidly. We use RoPE interpolation in the resolution transition. We keep a fixed grid aligned to 512² and multiply the 1024² patch indices by ½ to align them to the 512² coordinate axes. Moreover, we start the 1024² EMA 2M samples in, so it has adjusted to the new resolution before we start the averaging process. \n For post-training, we used a variant of Finite Difference Flow Optimization (FDFO) with JEV-Image and human-in-the-loop judges. \n The whole release cost about $21K of compute Pretraining $ 20,348 8,139 H100-hours × $2.50 610M samples at 512², then 50M at 1024² Post-training $ 690 276 H100-hours × $2.50 FDFO with JEV-Image and human-in-the-loop judges Total $ 21,038 $ 20,348 + $ 690 · 8,415 H100-hours pretraining on 64 H100s, restarts included priced at $2.50 per H100-hour \n It's still early days for post-training diffusion models To be blunt, post-training is a very young discipline within diffusion models, and we've only seen the first sparks of it working in the past few months. Of course there's been a lot of work in model and schedule distillation, but when it comes to post-training in terms of improving fundamental model capabilities (like we've seen with coding in LLMs over the past 18 months), it hasn't really come to fruition just yet for diffusion. DPO has been around for a while, but tends to dramatically overfit to specific aesthetic choices. Flow-GRPO was one of the first articulations of bringing RLVR-style frameworks to diffusion, and that only came out last fall. Since then there have been a few papers like Diffusion NFT and this June's FDFO (the best articulation of diffusion post-training so far, in our opinion). We think that this difficulty in adapting techniques comes down to the fact that we don't have access to strong likelihood estimates in diffusion models like we have in auto-regressive LLMs. We'll dive more into these concerns and our full post-training stack in the coming weeks. \n Model Limitations \n This model was not trained with images of rendered text (no infographics, PDFs, pages of books, PowerPoint slides), so it cannot write text or generate posters. As we detailed in our data filtration blog , we don't consider this data useful for our ultimate goal of animation tools, so this text-heavy data has been filtered out entirely from our dataset. \n This is a small model (2B-parameter DiT, 4B text encoder), and accordingly there are a few areas where the model struggles: full body, many entities (e.g. 6 cars, crowds), multi-person interactions, and world knowledge. The model is capable of correctly generating these images on occasion. Training a larger model (e.g. 7B parameters) and further post-training would likely mitigate these issues. \n Example Model Failures ‹ › Full body : legs deformed on the starting blocks \n Appendix A – Training Settings \n The schedule widens after 80M samples and stays wide through the 1024² stage training progress phase 2 from 80 M of 660 M samples share of training timesteps within 0.08 to 0.13 · hover the plot to move phase 1 · LogitNormal(0.8, 0.8) <0.05% phase 2 · LogitNormal(−0.2, 1.0) 3.0% phase 2 / phase 1 107× \n Every setting of the run, by stage Setting 512² stage 1024² stage Samples 610.27M (297,985 steps) 49.97M (24,400 steps) Global batch 2,048 2,048 Data Images at native 512px-class sizes: 368×512, 512×512, 512×384, 512×336, 640×360 Images at native 1024px-class sizes: 864×1216, 1024×1024, 1152×864, 1248×832, 1280×720 Aspect-ratio mix 20% each, no resizing 20% each, no resizing Noising : the noise scale doubles with the side length Timesteps LogitNormal(0.8, 0.8) for the first 80M samples (step 39,063), then LogitNormal(−0.2, 1.0) LogitNormal(−0.2, 1.0) throughout Objective x-prediction, v-loss, weight clamped at t = 0.1 · readouts at weight 1 · LPIPS 0.1 and P-DINO 0.01 on the final head for t ≤ 0.7 · PixelREPA 0.1 Optimizer Muon (momentum 0.95, Nesterov) on the 2D block matrices · AdamW (β = 0.9, 0.999, ε = 1e-8) on the rest · Muon updates RMS-matched to AdamW Learning rate Peak 2e-4 · linear warmup over 1M samples · flat to 550M · 1 − √ decay to 2.5e-5 over the last 60M Linear re-warm over 0.5M samples to 2.5e-5, then flat Weight decay 0.1 (0 on biases) Gradient clipping 1.0 (global norm) EMA Half-life 1,954 steps (4M samples), from 100M samples (step 48,829) Half-life 977 steps (2M samples), from 2M samples Spike-aware EMA EMA updates pause for 250 steps when the gradient norm exceeds 10× its rolling median Caption dropout 5%, for classifier-free guidance Hardware 64× H100 (8 nodes) · bf16 · FSDP2 · 32 images per GPU Same, as 4 accumulated microbatches of 8 \n For further details on the architecture and training losses, refer to the Appendix of P-JiT . \n Authorship statement \n We wrote all the words on this page. We used Claude Opus 5.5 to help us build the image gallery and diagrams. \n The Huggingface Model Card and Github Repo were written automatically by Claude Opus 5.5. We pointed Claude to our internal, experiment repo and had it pull out (and clean up) the necessary code. Get Field Notes Technical deep dives on building generative video models from the ground up, plus updates on new releases from Linum. Subscribe Field Notes · About © 2026 Linum, Inc. Privacy Terms @linuminc "
 },
 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50368208": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T20:13:59.396Z",
  "bytes": 3907,
  "text": " Show HN: A decent text-to-image model trained for ~$20K (Apache 2.0 Weights) | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login Show HN: A decent text-to-image model trained for ~$20K (Apache 2.0 Weights) ( linum.ai ) 79 points by schopra909 4 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 7 comments My brother and I run a 2-person lab that trains our own generative video models from scratch. The ultimate goal is a set of creative tools that make it easier for anyone to make animation. Video models today are too slow/expensive and lack sufficient controls. Creativity is an iterative process. You make, you tweak, you get better. In our opinion, they're not usable yet for storytelling. When we set out to build Linum v3 (our next video model) earlier this year, the primary goal was to bring the cost envelope of inference down significantly. If our model is fast and cheap, it's the first step (of many) towards useful creative tools. Typically, you see \"latent diffusion models\". A VAE (Variational Autoencoder) compresses the input data into a smaller \"latent space\" and the diffusion model learns how to generate within this subspace. Over the past few months, we've been publishing our work on efficient pixel-space diffusion models. These throw away the VAE, having the diffusion model learn a latent-space optimized for its exact task. This serves 3 purposes:\n1) Simplify Architecture (no 2-stage model training)\n2) Faster Convergence (11x faster convergence vs. our latent diffusion counterpart)\n3) Cheaper Inference (4x more aggressive token reduction in attention context-windows, without significant loss in quality) This past month we were pressure testing our latest architecture (Pyramid-JiT) by training a text-to-image model longer than the typical one-off experiment. Specifically, we trained it for ~$20K on 660M samples. We were surprised by the quality of results, so we thought we'd release it as open weights for the community (Apache 2.0). The research itself is already public, on our website. For us, it's a big stepping stone towards cheaper video models, since pretty much all video models start as image models. We like to say these models have to learn nouns before they learn verbs. help \n \n Robin_Message 9 minutes ago | next [–] \n What are you using as your training data out of interest? Very impressive generation. reply tensor 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n Amazing. I think this is an understudied areas. The big labs if anything are disincentivized to make efficient models as currently access to compute is one of their moats. That's not the same for Chinese labs, which is why you see groundbreaking efficiency work mostly coming from China. Really happy to see a small shop like yours doing this sort of work! reply Valk3_ 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n What was the most difficult challenge for you guys with this project? Also I'm a little intrigued on looking into efficiency techniques for video gen. What are current research directions in that field and promising directions you are particularly interested in? Any pointers for beginners? reply whythismatters 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n HF link: https://huggingface.co/Linum-AI/pjit-21k reply gunalx 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Interesting. It does however seem to struggle with a lot of the typical t2i models struggle with. \nOver smoothing, lots of uncanney shine, mangled limbs and such. reply dvh 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n How many ladybugs are in training dataset? reply gigel82 2 hours ago | prev [–] \n I wonder how this behaves when quantized, and how far are we from being able to run something like this purely in the browser with WebGPU. If you swap the text encoder for something like T5-small or CLIP, this could be <2.5Gb at INT4; doable on a lot of machines. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
